WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:12.440
Hello, everyone, and welcome
to lecture 5 of CME 296.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:18.240
So we are officially entering
the second half of this class.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:22.120
So we did a lot of hard
work during the first half.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:28.000
We derived a tractable loss to
learn how to generate images.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.600
So if you remember, the
first three lectures

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.040
was us looking from
different perspectives

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.080
to derive that loss.

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:40.580
And then something else we did,
which was, again, hard work,

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:45.380
was figuring out in which space
those images were living--

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.280
so how to represent images.

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.320
And so this second half is
going to be more practical.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.880
And this lecture is
specifically going

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.390
to be about the architectures
behind the image-generation

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.630
models.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:04.190
So before we start,
as usual, we're

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:08.550
going to recap what
we did the last times.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:12.990
So as I mentioned, the
first three lectures

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.390
were around deriving a
tractable training loss

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:19.830
to learn how to generate images.

00:01:19.830 --> 00:01:23.390
So what we want is to be
able to generate images

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:26.030
like the one that
is on the slides.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.950
And we saw that a
common denominator is us

00:01:29.950 --> 00:01:32.350
starting from those.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.050
So we saw three perspectives.

00:01:34.050 --> 00:01:38.390
The first one was
diffusion with DDPM.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:41.710
So we saw that we were
corrupting a clean image.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:45.270
And then we were learning
how to reconstruct it

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:49.470
by predicting the noise
that we had to remove.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:53.030
And so we ended up with a loss
that was actually quite nice--

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:56.630
L2 loss on the noise.

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:02.070
Then in lecture 2, we saw
another perspective, which was,

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:06.790
let's figure out the regions
of high density of the data

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:09.070
distribution of interest.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:14.630
And what we did was derive a
loss that is based on the score.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.830
The score here is the
gradient of the logarithm

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:21.270
of the probability distribution.

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.670
And then the last one was
lecture 3, where we actually

00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:27.150
said, OK, this
problem can actually

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.830
be framed as a
transport problem, where

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.710
we want to go from an initial
distribution, which is

00:02:33.710 --> 00:02:36.470
the noise, to the target one.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:41.110
And here we derive the loss
based on the velocity, a.k.a,

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.910
vector field, which
is again, very nice.

00:02:43.910 --> 00:02:48.790
It can be expressed as
an L2 regression loss.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:52.150
And in between, we have
this denoising process

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:58.660
that is similar in these three
paradigms but done differently.

00:02:58.660 --> 00:03:03.700
And then in lecture 4 we
said, OK, up until that point,

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:08.080
we just assumed that we were
able to represent images,

00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:11.360
the XTs, and we
said, OK, actually,

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:14.860
let's figure out a good
way to represent them

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.380
in a way that makes sense.

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:21.020
And what we said
was that we wanted

00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.300
them to be represented
in a space that was not

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:27.420
too high in dimensionality.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.540
So there was a space called
latent space, in which we want

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:33.220
our images to be represented.

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:36.780
And we want the representations
to be meaningful.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:38.160
So what does meaningful mean?

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:44.380
It means having some
structure to the space,

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:47.660
because what we
want is our model

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:52.980
to have an easy time learning
this generation process.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.580
And we focused on the VAE,
Variational Autoencoder,

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:03.980
which was structuring the latent
space by having some constraints

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:10.020
on how the distribution was
behaving over the latent space.

00:04:10.020 --> 00:04:12.840
So we saw this KL
divergence term in the loss.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:18.060
But then we said, OK, actually
the images were becoming blurry.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:21.339
So then we looked at
a number of strategies

00:04:21.339 --> 00:04:23.480
to combat the blurriness.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.400
So we saw the adversarial loss.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:29.180
We saw perceptual
loss and so on.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:30.020
So we're happy.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:33.100
Now we know how to
represent those images.

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:39.780
And we saw also that if we were
to condition our generation

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.140
process with the
condition, we also

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:48.060
had a method called
classifier free guidance that

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:54.930
allowed us to generate images by
emphasizing more on the prompts.

00:04:54.930 --> 00:05:01.530
And we did that by
predicting twice the,

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:07.250
let's say, noise to remove
or velocity or score.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:13.370
And with a guidance factor,
emphasize the generation process

00:05:13.370 --> 00:05:15.290
more towards the prompt.

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:19.210
So here this can be
represented on the image.

00:05:19.210 --> 00:05:22.450
And this is where we stopped.

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.690
And then, of course,
we had the midterm.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:27.370
And today, what
we're going to do

00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:33.330
is to focus on the architecture
behind the image-generation

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:41.170
model, which until now we
considered as being a black box.

00:05:41.170 --> 00:05:43.730
So I want to give a disclaimer.

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.730
There are many
different architectures.

00:05:46.730 --> 00:05:49.970
Our goal is not going
to just have a catalog

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.210
of every single one of them.

00:05:52.210 --> 00:05:54.690
But what I hope this
lecture will provide

00:05:54.690 --> 00:05:59.730
is at least an intuition as
to why certain components are

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:02.930
always being reused.

00:06:02.930 --> 00:06:07.090
So if you walk away with just
an understanding of why things

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.130
are the way they
are, then I think

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:14.570
it's going to be a
good thing for us.

00:06:14.570 --> 00:06:19.810
So let's first start
by what we want.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:22.530
So what we want,
if you remember,

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:26.690
diffusion, score
matching, flow matching,

00:06:26.690 --> 00:06:30.090
they had a few things as inputs.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:34.170
So first, they had x t--

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:37.090
the noisy image or
the noisy latent

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:41.170
that you're trying to denoise.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:45.210
You also had some indication
of the noise level

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:48.400
that's given by time step, t.

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.920
And you also have a condition.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:56.000
Let's call it C, which
it can be a text prompt.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.800
For instance, if
you want to generate

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:00.800
an image of a teddy
bear reading a book,

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:02.920
that can be your condition.

00:07:02.920 --> 00:07:07.600
So these three elements
are your inputs

00:07:07.600 --> 00:07:12.000
that goes through
your generation model.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:16.820
And as an output, you want
to predict, let's say,

00:07:16.820 --> 00:07:22.600
the velocity that will allow
you to then iteratively move

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:27.120
towards your desired
predicted image.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.120
And the goal of
this whole lecture

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.620
is to focus on that
generation model.

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:36.160
So that's the model that
we're talking about.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:39.160
But before we go
into that, I just

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:42.180
want to give a few slides notes.

00:07:42.180 --> 00:07:44.480
So the first one
is the condition.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.480
So I mentioned it can
be a piece of text.

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:53.850
In that case, such models are
called text to image models, T

00:07:53.850 --> 00:08:01.500
to I. But you can also have a
text and an image as input--

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.200
so an image editing
problem, in which case

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:09.520
the model will be called a
TI to I model, Text Image

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:10.880
to Image Model.

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:14.720
So here make this
teddy bear dance along

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:17.480
with the image of
the teddy bear.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:21.560
And then another thing is,
of course, the outputs.

00:08:21.560 --> 00:08:23.040
I mentioned there
are several ways

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.940
to parameterize your generation.

00:08:25.940 --> 00:08:30.020
You can either predict the
velocity or, of course,

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:31.960
the noise or the score.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:35.640
All of this is roughly
the same thing.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:38.023
But just for the
sake of consistency,

00:08:38.023 --> 00:08:39.440
we're just going
to say that we're

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.590
going to predict the velocity
just because nowadays

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.370
most models do that.

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:55.910
So with that in mind, what
do we want our model to be?

00:08:55.910 --> 00:09:02.470
So first we want our model
to be able to understand

00:09:02.470 --> 00:09:06.750
the global structure of the
image that it wants to generate.

00:09:06.750 --> 00:09:08.350
So that's one.

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:13.710
But we also wanted to understand
the nuances of the local details

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:18.830
so that it can also
generate crisp images.

00:09:18.830 --> 00:09:20.710
And of course, we
want a model that

00:09:20.710 --> 00:09:23.970
not only is able to
have x t and t as input,

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:27.890
but can also condition that
based on external signals.

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:32.630
And so here it can be
input text or input image.

00:09:32.630 --> 00:09:34.270
And we want, of
course, that model

00:09:34.270 --> 00:09:38.430
to be scalable because of
course, nowadays, we're

00:09:38.430 --> 00:09:41.910
generating images of
very high resolution,

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.630
and we're also generating
videos, which we have not

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:50.070
seen so far, but you can think
of this as being also applicable

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.990
there.

00:09:51.990 --> 00:09:57.550
So these are our wish lists.

00:09:57.550 --> 00:10:01.510
And let's start
with the first model

00:10:01.510 --> 00:10:08.350
by first thinking about how
humans look at an image.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:10.870
So you see this
teddy bear image.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:14.030
So you have an eye on the
right side of the slide.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:18.150
So when you look at this
image, what you're doing

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:22.190
is focusing on a specific
part of the image

00:10:22.190 --> 00:10:27.430
and skimming through that
image, scanning that image.

00:10:27.430 --> 00:10:31.590
That's what we do as humans.

00:10:31.590 --> 00:10:39.220
So one natural way of thinking
about how to build such a model

00:10:39.220 --> 00:10:45.300
is to have this bias.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:48.000
I will call it bias,
and I will justify it

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:49.800
why I say it that way.

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.460
We want to have
our model be biased

00:10:53.460 --> 00:10:58.620
towards looking at the image in
the way we humans look at it.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:02.980
I'm going to call that
bias, inductive bias.

00:11:02.980 --> 00:11:06.620
Who has heard of
inductive bias by the way?

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:07.900
Yeah, a few people.

00:11:07.900 --> 00:11:14.900
So we say that the model has an
inductive bias when we build it

00:11:14.900 --> 00:11:18.460
in such a way that it
behaves in some way

00:11:18.460 --> 00:11:21.460
that we think it should behave.

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.560
So in other words, here
we're going to say,

00:11:23.560 --> 00:11:28.540
OK, we want our model to
take some image as input,

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:33.900
and scan it a little
bit like humans do.

00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:35.620
And that's the
reason why I'm going

00:11:35.620 --> 00:11:39.620
to introduce an operation
that maybe you're already

00:11:39.620 --> 00:11:45.980
familiar with, which is called
the convolution operation, which

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:50.780
takes some inputs, let's
say an input image,

00:11:50.780 --> 00:11:55.980
and then uses a filter
to perform convolution

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:01.660
operation across that
input in order to generate

00:12:01.660 --> 00:12:06.780
or in order to
produce an output.

00:12:06.780 --> 00:12:10.340
So just on the terminology
side, the output

00:12:10.340 --> 00:12:15.980
is usually called feature
map or activation map.

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.940
And here this
convolution process

00:12:18.940 --> 00:12:24.140
typically leads to your
output exhibiting things

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:30.100
like edges, corners,
texture, from your inputs.

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:35.370
So it's going to extract
features, visual features.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:39.210
So another thing that I
will note is on this slide,

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:44.170
you may think the convolution
is done in a 2D manner,

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:49.210
but let's remember that images
are represented in height,

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:54.210
width, but also depth, because
you have the three values that

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:55.970
represent a pixel.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:02.290
And so the filter is as deep--

00:13:02.290 --> 00:13:06.330
if you want to think of it
that way-- as the input.

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:12.930
And so the convolution is really
across the depth axis as well.

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:16.450
So in particular, those
filters, they're 3D.

00:13:16.450 --> 00:13:20.090
They have a dimension
of F times F times C,

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:24.770
which is the same C as
the number of channels

00:13:24.770 --> 00:13:26.530
from the input.

00:13:26.530 --> 00:13:30.930
And we typically
have K such filters.

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:35.600
And then the number
of steps by which

00:13:35.600 --> 00:13:41.000
we move the filter
is called the stride.

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:45.560
So that's also a terminology
you should have in mind.

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:50.620
So based on that, let's
remember the conditions we had.

00:13:50.620 --> 00:13:53.420
We want our image to
understand global structure.

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:55.620
We want to preserve
local details.

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:57.180
We want external signals.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.200
And we want it to be scalable.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:05.000
Well, with that, at least we
have seemingly a tractable way

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:06.420
of doing this computation.

00:14:06.420 --> 00:14:10.440
So let's say that
number 4 is good.

00:14:10.440 --> 00:14:12.560
Local details, I
think that's also

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:18.120
something that is
valid here, because you

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:26.040
are able to capture patterns
within a certain range of,

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:26.900
let's say, pixels.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:29.240
So that one is also good.

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:32.120
But how about the
global structure?

00:14:32.120 --> 00:14:35.880
You want your output-- so
here you want your output

00:14:35.880 --> 00:14:44.080
to have seen the whole input
to be able to be aware of it.

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:49.320
So there is this concept
of a receptive field, which

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:54.400
is defined by the area
that an activation

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:57.920
element is able to see.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:02.000
So here, let's suppose
we have an input that

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:04.760
has a series of convolutions.

00:15:04.760 --> 00:15:07.760
So what we're saying
is if we take just one

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:12.480
element from the output, we
want to know how many elements

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:16.320
from the input it has seen.

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:18.440
So this is the receptive field.

00:15:18.440 --> 00:15:21.320
So there is some
formulas there that I

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:23.120
don't want us to just
remember, but I just

00:15:23.120 --> 00:15:29.830
want to call out that the
area that an element can see

00:15:29.830 --> 00:15:37.590
is a function of the filter
dimension and the strides.

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:46.430
And if you want the output
to have seen all the input,

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:52.270
you can imagine that you
need a lot of convolutions,

00:15:52.270 --> 00:15:54.830
because here in this
slide, if you do,

00:15:54.830 --> 00:15:59.990
let's say, just one convolution,
then the area is just,

00:15:59.990 --> 00:16:02.750
let's say, five.

00:16:02.750 --> 00:16:05.070
So you do two
convolutions actually.

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:11.370
But images, they are typically
500 by 500, 1,000 by 1,000.

00:16:11.370 --> 00:16:15.550
So you need a lot of
such convolutions,

00:16:15.550 --> 00:16:18.390
which may not be as tractable.

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:25.870
So one idea that we can have is
to have down-sampling operations

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:30.630
that would allow us was to have
a bigger receptive field early

00:16:30.630 --> 00:16:37.550
on so that we can have a global
understanding of the structure,

00:16:37.550 --> 00:16:43.990
and then we can have some
up-sampling operations to then

00:16:43.990 --> 00:16:48.830
end up with the same
dimensions as the input.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:51.450
That's just an idea
I'm throwing out,

00:16:51.450 --> 00:16:53.990
because let's remember
that our model needs

00:16:53.990 --> 00:16:57.430
to have the inputs X--

00:16:57.430 --> 00:17:00.230
so the noisy image as input.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.750
And it needs to
return something that

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:06.790
is as the same dimension
as the input as well.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:10.430
So you cannot just reduce the
dimension and just call it

00:17:10.430 --> 00:17:11.089
a day.

00:17:11.089 --> 00:17:13.230
You cannot do that.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:16.109
So for that reason,
we're going to see

00:17:16.109 --> 00:17:21.550
a series of operations
that are going to help us

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:23.910
in building such a model.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:28.980
So here let's see a very
common down-sampling operation.

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:31.700
So who has heard
of pooling here?

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:32.660
Pooling?

00:17:32.660 --> 00:17:33.240
Great.

00:17:33.240 --> 00:17:38.080
So pooling, as you know, is you
take, let's say a given square,

00:17:38.080 --> 00:17:40.580
and what you do is you
just down-sample it

00:17:40.580 --> 00:17:45.860
by either taking the max value
or just the average value.

00:17:45.860 --> 00:17:50.500
So there is no learnable
parameters there.

00:17:50.500 --> 00:17:52.920
And speaking of
learnable parameters,

00:17:52.920 --> 00:17:57.100
I may have been a bit too fast
there, but here the filters that

00:17:57.100 --> 00:18:01.440
are used for convolutional
operation, they, of course,

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:02.820
are learnable.

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:08.580
And you have F times F times
C parameters per filter

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:10.300
plus some bias
term, if you assume

00:18:10.300 --> 00:18:17.720
that there is bias, times K. Not
that I want to go to formulas,

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:19.940
but just giving you a
sense of what is learned

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:22.100
and what is not learned.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:24.160
So pooling, you don't
learn anything--

00:18:24.160 --> 00:18:27.380
just the pooling operation.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:31.640
So this is just
down-sampling operations.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:36.020
But I mentioned we want to
also be able to up-sample.

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:42.460
And there is an operation called
the transpose convolution that

00:18:42.460 --> 00:18:44.400
we're not going to go
into too much detail,

00:18:44.400 --> 00:18:48.540
but I just want to call out
that there is an up-sampling

00:18:48.540 --> 00:18:55.620
operation, that is the transpose
convolution that uses filters

00:18:55.620 --> 00:19:02.260
to broadcast elements from
the input towards the output--

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:06.260
so just up-samples it.

00:19:06.260 --> 00:19:16.180
So given that, I hope it's not
coming out of the blue, that's

00:19:16.180 --> 00:19:19.740
a model that
down-samples the input

00:19:19.740 --> 00:19:25.610
and then up-samples it could
be a natural choice of such

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:28.250
a model, because here
what you're doing

00:19:28.250 --> 00:19:31.570
is during the
down-sampling step,

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:35.930
you're able to have receptive
fields of your output

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:41.450
that sees most of the inputs,
which is the global structure

00:19:41.450 --> 00:19:43.850
understanding part.

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:45.910
And then you have the
up-sampling part--

00:19:45.910 --> 00:19:47.810
so the one on the right--

00:19:47.810 --> 00:19:54.810
that just increases your output
once again back to the dimension

00:19:54.810 --> 00:19:57.730
that it was just
in order for you

00:19:57.730 --> 00:20:02.610
to have a shape that's
abides by what you want.

00:20:02.610 --> 00:20:07.210
And here you can also
have direct connections

00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:14.010
from one to the other to just
transport some local details

00:20:14.010 --> 00:20:18.610
that you may have computed.

00:20:18.610 --> 00:20:24.050
So such a model
is called U-Net--

00:20:24.050 --> 00:20:25.810
well, because it's
a shape like a U

00:20:25.810 --> 00:20:29.890
when you do the illustration.

00:20:29.890 --> 00:20:33.950
And so I mentioned a little
bit of the intuition.

00:20:33.950 --> 00:20:36.030
But we're going to
go step by step.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:41.170
So the first step is
the down-sampling step.

00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:47.490
So it has a combination of
convolutions and pooling

00:20:47.490 --> 00:20:54.690
that are able to down-sample the
dimension of the feature map.

00:20:54.690 --> 00:20:58.370
And as we saw, it just
allows your output

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:01.430
to have seen more and
more of the input.

00:21:01.430 --> 00:21:02.970
And when I say
that, what I mean is

00:21:02.970 --> 00:21:07.570
if you take a single pixel,
a single activation value,

00:21:07.570 --> 00:21:10.950
it has seen more and
more of the input--

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.490
a bigger area of the input.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:15.170
So in that sense,
what I'm saying is,

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:21.800
it is able to understand the
global structure of the inputs.

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:24.060
Is everyone clear
on that statement?

00:21:27.640 --> 00:21:28.140
Cool.

00:21:28.140 --> 00:21:29.940
So this is similar
to an encoder.

00:21:29.940 --> 00:21:32.340
And then you have
the second step,

00:21:32.340 --> 00:21:35.640
which is the up-sampling part.

00:21:35.640 --> 00:21:39.040
So you have here a feature
map at the very beginning

00:21:39.040 --> 00:21:43.360
of the up-sampling step,
which is very small.

00:21:43.360 --> 00:21:48.280
And what you want is to
up-sample that feature map back

00:21:48.280 --> 00:21:51.000
to the dimension of the input.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:55.840
So here you're using a mix
of transpose convolution also

00:21:55.840 --> 00:21:59.800
called up convolution
and convolutions.

00:21:59.800 --> 00:22:03.520
And you can think of
this more as a decoder.

00:22:03.520 --> 00:22:07.280
So it just leverages
the understanding,

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:09.160
the global structure
understanding

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:13.000
that is compressed
in the representation

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:16.600
at the very bottom, which
is called the bottleneck.

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:22.880
And it just gets it back to the
same dimension as the input.

00:22:22.880 --> 00:22:27.820
But at that step, you
may tell me, well, sure,

00:22:27.820 --> 00:22:30.820
you can have a global
understanding of the structure,

00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:33.960
but you may lose the details.

00:22:33.960 --> 00:22:39.880
And that's where these
connections come into play.

00:22:39.880 --> 00:22:43.800
So during the down-sampling
step, what you want

00:22:43.800 --> 00:22:48.440
is to leverage the patterns
that you're computing

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:50.620
in the up-sampling step.

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:56.880
So what you're doing is
you're taking at each level

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:02.920
a part of the feature map, and
you're just simply copying--

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:07.360
so cropping and copying.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:10.520
So that just allows
local details

00:23:10.520 --> 00:23:13.560
to flow from what
you have computed

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:16.970
in the down-sampling step
to the up-sampling step.

00:23:19.910 --> 00:23:21.590
Is that part clear?

00:23:21.590 --> 00:23:23.613
So the question is,
what are you copying?

00:23:23.613 --> 00:23:24.530
What are you cropping?

00:23:24.530 --> 00:23:26.630
So great question.

00:23:26.630 --> 00:23:31.670
So what you're doing here
is the feature map that

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:34.670
you're computing in
the down-sampling step,

00:23:34.670 --> 00:23:37.270
you're just literally
taking that--

00:23:37.270 --> 00:23:42.350
literally doing that-- and
then the feature map that

00:23:42.350 --> 00:23:45.270
is part of the
up-sampling step, you're

00:23:45.270 --> 00:23:48.790
just concatenating
those features

00:23:48.790 --> 00:23:53.710
to the features that are
in the up-sampling step.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:57.750
So in other words, in the
up-sampling procedure,

00:23:57.750 --> 00:23:59.550
you may have a feature
map that you're

00:23:59.550 --> 00:24:05.350
computing that is let's say
of size O times O times C.

00:24:05.350 --> 00:24:09.350
So what you're saying here is
you're concatenating something

00:24:09.350 --> 00:24:14.950
that is also of size O
times O times C, where

00:24:14.950 --> 00:24:18.990
maybe the C can be
different, but you're just

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:21.830
concatenating the two.

00:24:21.830 --> 00:24:24.390
So of course, you have some
variations of how this is done,

00:24:24.390 --> 00:24:28.290
but this is what this
illustration represents.

00:24:31.070 --> 00:24:34.030
So the question is,
why are you not looking

00:24:34.030 --> 00:24:35.890
at the down-sampled features?

00:24:35.890 --> 00:24:38.950
Because we have already
lost the details already.

00:24:38.950 --> 00:24:41.310
So if you see on
the slide, we're

00:24:41.310 --> 00:24:44.350
actually copying and
cropping the features

00:24:44.350 --> 00:24:49.830
that are before we're at that
very down-sampled outputs.

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:53.570
Because as you go down, you
lose more and more details,

00:24:53.570 --> 00:24:57.110
but then you're gaining global
understanding of the inputs.

00:24:57.110 --> 00:24:59.150
And so that's why you
want your connections

00:24:59.150 --> 00:25:04.190
to go from the very top to
the other way and so on.

00:25:04.190 --> 00:25:06.530
Yeah.

00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:08.890
So the question is, how
we're training this model.

00:25:08.890 --> 00:25:11.260
So we're training it jointly.

00:25:11.260 --> 00:25:13.200
So we're not going to
go into details there,

00:25:13.200 --> 00:25:17.540
but let's just assume you can
train the whole thing by just

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:21.460
considering it as just a model.

00:25:21.460 --> 00:25:24.620
So the question is,
can the model output

00:25:24.620 --> 00:25:26.600
exactly what the input image?

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:30.060
So that is not what
you want because-- so I

00:25:30.060 --> 00:25:33.220
think you're maybe remembering
the autoencoder that we

00:25:33.220 --> 00:25:34.900
saw last time.

00:25:34.900 --> 00:25:38.900
So the goal of
the autoencoder is

00:25:38.900 --> 00:25:43.020
to learn a representation
of the inputs and compress

00:25:43.020 --> 00:25:45.260
it into in a latent
space so that you

00:25:45.260 --> 00:25:48.700
can sample from the latent
space and then reconstruct it.

00:25:48.700 --> 00:25:51.220
And the problem here is
you have the connections

00:25:51.220 --> 00:25:55.380
that come from the thing that
you're not supposed to have.

00:25:55.380 --> 00:26:01.620
So you can think of this
model as taking a noisy latent

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:02.740
as input.

00:26:02.740 --> 00:26:06.060
But your goal is to not
reconstruct that output,

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:09.980
it's actually to
predict the thing that

00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:13.140
will allow you to denoise it.

00:26:13.140 --> 00:26:15.100
So in that sense, there
are some similarities

00:26:15.100 --> 00:26:19.580
with an autoencoder,
but it's very different.

00:26:19.580 --> 00:26:20.540
Yep.

00:26:20.540 --> 00:26:23.180
So the question is, does
the output of the model

00:26:23.180 --> 00:26:26.600
have to be equal to the
dimensions of the input?

00:26:26.600 --> 00:26:27.660
Yes.

00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:30.100
Because so the question is, why?

00:26:30.100 --> 00:26:35.140
Well if you take your
input latent x t,

00:26:35.140 --> 00:26:37.620
so if you remember back
in lecture 3, which

00:26:37.620 --> 00:26:39.860
was like two or
three weeks ago, we

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:45.140
had the iterative
step, which was,

00:26:45.140 --> 00:26:54.918
sorry xt plus dt is equal to
xt plus the velocity times dt.

00:26:54.918 --> 00:26:56.460
So if you just look
at that equation,

00:26:56.460 --> 00:27:01.380
you just see that the
dimensions need to match.

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:05.100
Another way to think about
it is in the diffusion

00:27:05.100 --> 00:27:08.330
with the DPM formulation.

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:12.510
What you want is to find
the noise to remove,

00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:16.090
and that noise needs to
match your input image.

00:27:16.090 --> 00:27:19.690
Exactly, exactly.

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:22.090
OK, perfect.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.130
I have a lot of content.

00:27:24.130 --> 00:27:27.270
So we'll try to move
a little bit faster,

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:29.770
but I try to also
do some pauses here

00:27:29.770 --> 00:27:34.010
and there to make sure
we're still together now.

00:27:34.010 --> 00:27:36.070
So you have that model.

00:27:36.070 --> 00:27:39.050
But now you may wonder,
well, how can you

00:27:39.050 --> 00:27:41.390
add external conditions?

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:44.670
How can you inform your model
that you're at time step t?

00:27:44.670 --> 00:27:47.970
How can you inform your model
that you want to condition it

00:27:47.970 --> 00:27:50.010
with respect to some condition?

00:27:50.010 --> 00:27:52.850
Well, that's a great question.

00:27:52.850 --> 00:27:56.130
And so to answer that
question, you first

00:27:56.130 --> 00:28:03.210
need to think about how you want
to represent your time step t

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:05.360
and your condition C.

00:28:05.360 --> 00:28:08.120
Well, let's start
with the time step.

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:13.240
Here the goal is to represent
the time step in a D dimensional

00:28:13.240 --> 00:28:15.840
space.

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:19.340
And before I tell
you how we do it,

00:28:19.340 --> 00:28:23.360
I just want you to
look at your watch.

00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:28.800
Well, as humans,
we construct time

00:28:28.800 --> 00:28:31.800
as a function of
different dimensions--

00:28:31.800 --> 00:28:38.240
hours, minutes, seconds,
days, and so on and so forth.

00:28:38.240 --> 00:28:42.960
And you can think of
each of these dimensions

00:28:42.960 --> 00:28:46.480
as being part of a vector
if you think about it.

00:28:46.480 --> 00:28:49.160
And one thing that's
quite interesting

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:52.400
about these dimensions
is that they

00:28:52.400 --> 00:28:55.760
run at different frequencies.

00:28:55.760 --> 00:28:59.030
So for instance, an hour
is kind of slow to pass.

00:28:59.030 --> 00:29:00.780
So I hope, by the way,
this lecture is fun

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:05.720
so you're not feeling like an
hour is low, but hour is slow.

00:29:05.720 --> 00:29:07.760
Minute is faster.

00:29:07.760 --> 00:29:09.840
Seconds are even faster.

00:29:09.840 --> 00:29:11.780
So you have a difference
in frequencies,

00:29:11.780 --> 00:29:17.400
a difference in frequencies,
which hopefully motivates

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:25.160
the fact that what we want is
to represent these time steps

00:29:25.160 --> 00:29:30.420
as vectors of quantities
that are also sinusoidal--

00:29:30.420 --> 00:29:34.680
so things that have
some frequency to it--

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:38.940
where some dimensions are,
let's say, slower than others.

00:29:41.880 --> 00:29:46.080
So is that motivating example
intuitive enough for this

00:29:46.080 --> 00:29:47.880
to be making sense?

00:29:47.880 --> 00:29:50.000
So in other words,
let me tell you what

00:29:50.000 --> 00:29:52.840
that illustration represents.

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:57.960
If you take a single
row, then what you have

00:29:57.960 --> 00:30:03.150
is an illustration of the
values of each dimension.

00:30:03.150 --> 00:30:07.270
So what that means is, for
instance, at the very bottom,

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:11.230
or let's start at the
very top, let's say.

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:15.710
You have values that alternate
between let's say 1 and 0

00:30:15.710 --> 00:30:16.690
in this example.

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:18.190
And then as you
move down, you see

00:30:18.190 --> 00:30:23.350
that some dimensions they
vary faster than others.

00:30:23.350 --> 00:30:29.470
And this is exactly matching
the intuition I mentioned.

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:32.550
So that is for the time step.

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:34.710
Now the condition.

00:30:34.710 --> 00:30:37.670
So I mentioned a condition
can be a piece of text.

00:30:37.670 --> 00:30:41.390
Well, you have a number of
ways to represent that text.

00:30:41.390 --> 00:30:45.310
We've seen some methods in the
past lecture in terms of how

00:30:45.310 --> 00:30:47.870
to represent text and image.

00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:52.290
But here, I'll just mention just
a few categories of methods.

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:58.230
So there's one where you have
very basic predefined class.

00:30:58.230 --> 00:31:02.550
And what you're doing is
you're just learning the vector

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:05.430
representation of that class.

00:31:05.430 --> 00:31:10.350
Or you can go more granular
and divide your input text

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.550
into tokens and then
have representation

00:31:12.550 --> 00:31:14.550
for each of these tokens.

00:31:14.550 --> 00:31:16.910
So for both these
categories of methods,

00:31:16.910 --> 00:31:19.410
you have a bunch
of such methods.

00:31:19.410 --> 00:31:21.510
But one very common
method these days

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:23.990
is just to get a
pre-trained LLM,

00:31:23.990 --> 00:31:27.590
and just grab the
embeddings, which

00:31:27.590 --> 00:31:32.790
we will not go into details here
but what people do nowadays.

00:31:32.790 --> 00:31:35.030
And then for the
first method, we

00:31:35.030 --> 00:31:39.230
can simply take
a VIT-based model

00:31:39.230 --> 00:31:43.670
and just take the
representation of the CLS

00:31:43.670 --> 00:31:49.470
token, which I believe we
briefly saw in the last lecture.

00:31:49.470 --> 00:31:56.980
All of that to say that we have
ways to represent the condition.

00:31:56.980 --> 00:31:57.600
Cool.

00:31:57.600 --> 00:32:00.600
So let's suppose we have a
representation of the time step.

00:32:00.600 --> 00:32:03.620
We have a representation
of the condition.

00:32:03.620 --> 00:32:08.540
Now, how do you inject
it in your model?

00:32:08.540 --> 00:32:11.580
Well, here again, you
have a number of methods.

00:32:11.580 --> 00:32:14.020
There is no consensus.

00:32:14.020 --> 00:32:18.420
One is injecting that
to the feature map.

00:32:18.420 --> 00:32:22.000
So here what I mean is
you take your vector t,

00:32:22.000 --> 00:32:26.940
you take your vector c,
let's say you add them,

00:32:26.940 --> 00:32:29.740
and then you make sure that
the dimension of your vector

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:34.260
is, let's say, c the
number of channels.

00:32:34.260 --> 00:32:38.020
And then what you do is you
add it to your feature map,

00:32:38.020 --> 00:32:41.780
which if you remember
is of depth c.

00:32:41.780 --> 00:32:44.380
So that can be one way.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:48.260
Another way is to
modulate that feature.

00:32:48.260 --> 00:32:51.780
And that part we will see
that in about 20 minutes.

00:32:51.780 --> 00:32:55.100
So just wait for that.

00:32:55.100 --> 00:33:01.460
And then the third one is you do
some cross attention operation

00:33:01.460 --> 00:33:06.380
there, which we're going to see
as well, maybe in 40 minutes.

00:33:06.380 --> 00:33:10.820
So all of that to say that
there are a number of ways

00:33:10.820 --> 00:33:14.860
to inject TNC, and there
is not like a standard way

00:33:14.860 --> 00:33:15.800
that people do it.

00:33:15.800 --> 00:33:18.660
So this is just a
way for me to tell

00:33:18.660 --> 00:33:22.700
you there are several methods.

00:33:22.700 --> 00:33:27.580
So in terms of
timeline, we're in 2026.

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:32.060
So just giving you a sense
of when these models were

00:33:32.060 --> 00:33:34.940
published when these were used.

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:39.420
So in 2015, the
U-Net paper came out

00:33:39.420 --> 00:33:43.480
in the context of image
segmentation for, I believe,

00:33:43.480 --> 00:33:46.020
medical images.

00:33:46.020 --> 00:33:50.220
But it is something that has not
been widely in use in the image

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:54.530
generation field up
until the early 2020s.

00:33:54.530 --> 00:33:57.875
So for instance, you
have the DDPM paper,

00:33:57.875 --> 00:33:59.250
which if you
remember, we focused

00:33:59.250 --> 00:34:02.330
on during lecture one
that actually used U-Net

00:34:02.330 --> 00:34:05.370
as an architecture.

00:34:05.370 --> 00:34:08.409
And then another thing
that's quite noteworthy

00:34:08.409 --> 00:34:14.530
is latent diffusion model, which
was a paper that introduced

00:34:14.530 --> 00:34:20.489
or that popularized the fact of
generating images in the latent

00:34:20.489 --> 00:34:25.810
space, also used some
form of the U-Net.

00:34:25.810 --> 00:34:29.130
And then we also had
a U-Net that was quite

00:34:29.130 --> 00:34:33.710
scaled in a very big way
in Stable Diffusion, Excel,

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:38.449
which was published in 2023.

00:34:38.449 --> 00:34:41.810
So if you look at that
timeline and if you

00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:47.070
look at my explanations, you
may think, well, that's great.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:48.130
We have everything.

00:34:48.130 --> 00:34:49.370
Let's stop here.

00:34:49.370 --> 00:34:52.130
We have our model.

00:34:52.130 --> 00:35:00.170
Well, not so fast, because
in 2017 and in 2020,

00:35:00.170 --> 00:35:05.103
there are papers that were
landmark papers that really

00:35:05.103 --> 00:35:06.770
revolutionize, for
instance, transformer

00:35:06.770 --> 00:35:10.090
revolutionized the NLP field?

00:35:10.090 --> 00:35:14.810
And one question you may
wonder is, well, they're

00:35:14.810 --> 00:35:17.530
performing so well there.

00:35:17.530 --> 00:35:18.610
What about here?

00:35:18.610 --> 00:35:20.610
What about us?

00:35:20.610 --> 00:35:22.370
And that's what the
vision transformer

00:35:22.370 --> 00:35:27.810
did in the image
understanding space, which

00:35:27.810 --> 00:35:31.810
took a backbone
of the transformer

00:35:31.810 --> 00:35:35.530
and applied it in the
image-understanding case

00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:38.170
and saw great results.

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:41.130
And so now you may wonder,
OK, but can we do the same

00:35:41.130 --> 00:35:44.570
for image-generation?

00:35:44.570 --> 00:35:48.560
And that's what we're
going to see now.

00:35:48.560 --> 00:35:51.400
But before we do
that, I told you

00:35:51.400 --> 00:35:54.620
I will try to not give you
things out of the blue.

00:35:54.620 --> 00:35:56.580
I'll try to build
some intuition.

00:35:56.580 --> 00:36:01.600
And here, one thing I
want to try to tell you

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:05.940
is, why would such a model
be beneficial for us?

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:08.140
Because we have the global
structure understanding.

00:36:08.140 --> 00:36:09.960
We have the local
details preserving.

00:36:09.960 --> 00:36:11.040
We have everything.

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:13.760
Why would we need that?

00:36:13.760 --> 00:36:17.520
Well, imagine if
you want to generate

00:36:17.520 --> 00:36:22.600
an image of a teddy
bear next to a mirror.

00:36:22.600 --> 00:36:26.520
Well, what happens
is in your image,

00:36:26.520 --> 00:36:31.800
what you need is for
the low level texture

00:36:31.800 --> 00:36:34.920
to be very similar in
one part of the image

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:37.940
and in a very different
part of that same image.

00:36:41.000 --> 00:36:46.720
So the problem with the U-Net
is you have convolutions

00:36:46.720 --> 00:36:50.240
that are localized to
in the neighborhood

00:36:50.240 --> 00:36:53.200
in the vicinity of pixels.

00:36:53.200 --> 00:36:57.180
But as you go further
from that pixel,

00:36:57.180 --> 00:36:59.200
you have a bunch of
information that gets

00:36:59.200 --> 00:37:01.840
mixed in whatever you compute.

00:37:01.840 --> 00:37:07.920
So you don't have almost pixel
to pixel or patches of pixel

00:37:07.920 --> 00:37:11.160
to patches of pixel interaction.

00:37:11.160 --> 00:37:17.560
So you're not able to
cleanly capture or to cleanly

00:37:17.560 --> 00:37:21.320
have an understanding of a local
detail in one part of the image

00:37:21.320 --> 00:37:24.800
and local detail in
another part of the image.

00:37:24.800 --> 00:37:28.920
And so this is a kind of example
that the U-Net may actually

00:37:28.920 --> 00:37:31.240
fail.

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:34.840
And so for that
reason, the transformer

00:37:34.840 --> 00:37:38.520
is actually a great
model for that because--

00:37:38.520 --> 00:37:39.620
yeah, exactly.

00:37:39.620 --> 00:37:41.840
So the transformer is
based on the concept

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:47.430
of attention, which was
initially introduced for texts.

00:37:47.430 --> 00:37:53.430
I'm going to briefly mention
it here, but the idea for text

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:58.950
was you want to compute
representations of your words

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:02.950
as a function of
all other words.

00:38:02.950 --> 00:38:08.470
So what you want is to have
direct links from one word

00:38:08.470 --> 00:38:12.510
to another word,
regardless of the distance.

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:20.350
So what you have is a concept
of queries, keys, and values.

00:38:20.350 --> 00:38:23.690
So what you're wondering
is, if you have a query,

00:38:23.690 --> 00:38:28.510
if you have a text, you
want to know how relevant

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:32.350
or how similar all the
other pieces of text,

00:38:32.350 --> 00:38:35.510
a.k.a tokens are.

00:38:35.510 --> 00:38:40.750
So you want to see how the
query relates two keys.

00:38:40.750 --> 00:38:43.510
And then once you how
relevant they are, so you

00:38:43.510 --> 00:38:46.230
take some separation of
the two-- so let's say

00:38:46.230 --> 00:38:49.310
dot product that's
normalized in a good way.

00:38:49.310 --> 00:38:53.470
And then you can take
the associated value.

00:38:53.470 --> 00:38:55.510
So that's the
attention mechanism

00:38:55.510 --> 00:38:58.150
or the self-attention
mechanism that is aimed

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:02.430
at removing the inductive bias.

00:39:02.430 --> 00:39:07.550
And here we can actually use
that same mechanism for images.

00:39:07.550 --> 00:39:15.437
So you can think that you
take pieces of your image

00:39:15.437 --> 00:39:16.770
and you're doing the same thing.

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:21.790
So you say, OK, a
given a set piece

00:39:21.790 --> 00:39:27.510
of image, what are the other
pieces of the same image that

00:39:27.510 --> 00:39:29.630
are relevant to that one?

00:39:29.630 --> 00:39:33.750
So it's the same mechanism.

00:39:33.750 --> 00:39:39.070
So what I told you also happens
to be very nicely represented

00:39:39.070 --> 00:39:42.380
mathematically-- so very
tractable computationally

00:39:42.380 --> 00:39:45.060
with the hardware
that we have today.

00:39:45.060 --> 00:39:50.500
So you may have seen the formula
softmax of Q times K transpose

00:39:50.500 --> 00:39:52.300
over some normalization
normalizing

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:57.980
constant times V. So that is the
self-attention operation just

00:39:57.980 --> 00:40:00.580
in matrix form.

00:40:00.580 --> 00:40:02.200
And on the right
side of the slide,

00:40:02.200 --> 00:40:04.740
you have just the
transformer architecture

00:40:04.740 --> 00:40:09.500
as it was initially introduced
in the context of I believe

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:11.540
it was translation--
so for instance,

00:40:11.540 --> 00:40:14.380
English to French translation.

00:40:14.380 --> 00:40:16.260
So you had two parts--

00:40:16.260 --> 00:40:18.700
one on the left side, which
is called the encoder, which

00:40:18.700 --> 00:40:23.620
computes rich embeddings of
your input, and the right side

00:40:23.620 --> 00:40:28.980
is the decoder that allows you
to do translation one at a time,

00:40:28.980 --> 00:40:34.540
by allowing each of your output
to interact to output that you

00:40:34.540 --> 00:40:39.770
decoded so far, to interact
with the whole input.

00:40:39.770 --> 00:40:42.850
So four images.

00:40:42.850 --> 00:40:45.530
We're not translating
anything, we're just

00:40:45.530 --> 00:40:47.910
processing pieces of images.

00:40:47.910 --> 00:40:52.130
So we're only going
to borrow the encoder

00:40:52.130 --> 00:40:56.370
part of the transformer--
so the left part.

00:40:56.370 --> 00:41:02.130
And that's what we saw the
vision transformer did.

00:41:02.130 --> 00:41:06.970
So it took just the encoder
part of the transformer,

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:11.530
and it took an
image as an input,

00:41:11.530 --> 00:41:13.970
and it did something very
special to that image

00:41:13.970 --> 00:41:15.550
as a pre-processing step.

00:41:15.550 --> 00:41:21.490
So what it did was
cut it into patches,

00:41:21.490 --> 00:41:24.130
and then consider
these patches as tokens

00:41:24.130 --> 00:41:27.930
in the same way it
is done for words,

00:41:27.930 --> 00:41:33.430
and then process them again with
the self-attention mechanism,

00:41:33.430 --> 00:41:39.010
allowing each patch of image
to interact with all other ones

00:41:39.010 --> 00:41:44.610
to just figure out which
ones are actually relevant.

00:41:44.610 --> 00:41:47.610
So this was the DiT.

00:41:47.610 --> 00:41:53.330
And let me introduce you to the
vision generation equivalent,

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:57.530
the diffusion transformer,
which it was introduced

00:41:57.530 --> 00:42:05.490
in 2022, which is also relying
on the self-attention mechanism.

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:08.050
And we're going to
see exactly how.

00:42:08.050 --> 00:42:10.850
But the idea is very similar.

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:16.810
You take a noisy latent as
input, and what you do is--

00:42:16.810 --> 00:42:18.630
so remember the wish
list that we had.

00:42:18.630 --> 00:42:20.790
We want to understand
global structure.

00:42:20.790 --> 00:42:24.730
We want to preserve local
details and so on and so forth.

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:29.450
So here what we're saying is
we're just allowing all patches

00:42:29.450 --> 00:42:31.190
to interact with one another.

00:42:34.560 --> 00:42:36.020
Does that make sense so far?

00:42:39.360 --> 00:42:42.340
It's not out of the blue
that I brought up this model.

00:42:42.340 --> 00:42:42.840
No.

00:42:42.840 --> 00:42:44.840
All good?

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:46.160
Perfect.

00:42:46.160 --> 00:42:54.440
So let's go step by step into
what each component means here.

00:42:54.440 --> 00:43:00.560
So you have on the illustration,
a patchy file operation,

00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:04.920
that takes in your
noised latent and that

00:43:04.920 --> 00:43:07.000
divides it into patches.

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:12.320
So you have let's say, I times
I times C tensor that you divide

00:43:12.320 --> 00:43:16.560
into P times P times C patches.

00:43:16.560 --> 00:43:22.510
And those patches you just want
to consider them as tokens.

00:43:22.510 --> 00:43:28.620
And you end up with an input
length of I over P squared.

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:33.840
So the smaller your
patches, the higher

00:43:33.840 --> 00:43:37.600
or the longer your
sequence, which

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:42.240
also matches your intuition.

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:46.200
So in that sense, you can
also tell from this step

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:49.560
that there is a trade off
between how granular you

00:43:49.560 --> 00:43:54.060
want to be and how much
computation you want to compute.

00:43:56.880 --> 00:43:57.780
So that's for that.

00:43:57.780 --> 00:44:00.520
And then the second step
is to actually inject

00:44:00.520 --> 00:44:03.600
your conditions-- so the
external signals, the time

00:44:03.600 --> 00:44:07.200
step, and then the
input text, let's say.

00:44:07.200 --> 00:44:13.400
So here what we do is we take
our timestep embedding, which

00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:19.000
as we saw can be a series
of sinusoidal functions

00:44:19.000 --> 00:44:21.920
that you have in
a vector, and then

00:44:21.920 --> 00:44:24.780
your condition that can
be just an embedding.

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:27.400
So I believe in the
DiT paper, they just

00:44:27.400 --> 00:44:31.110
consider the embedding of the
whole text-- so just one vector

00:44:31.110 --> 00:44:33.830
for the whole text.

00:44:33.830 --> 00:44:38.510
So let's suppose you have
these two quantities.

00:44:38.510 --> 00:44:41.970
Now the question is, how do you
inject that into your model?

00:44:45.190 --> 00:44:50.090
So the paper went through
different methods.

00:44:50.090 --> 00:44:52.190
But there was one
that stood out.

00:44:52.190 --> 00:44:55.710
So what we're going to do
is for me to just tell you

00:44:55.710 --> 00:44:58.230
what methods they
tried out and then

00:44:58.230 --> 00:45:01.390
focus on the one that
performed the best.

00:45:01.390 --> 00:45:06.390
So the first method was
called adaptive layer norm,

00:45:06.390 --> 00:45:11.710
which consisted of taking
the external signals

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:15.530
and then modulating the tokens.

00:45:15.530 --> 00:45:18.530
So do you remember when I told
you modulating in the U-Net?

00:45:18.530 --> 00:45:22.590
So that's the same modulating
modulation I'm talking about.

00:45:22.590 --> 00:45:25.350
So that's the first method.

00:45:25.350 --> 00:45:30.030
The second method is to say, OK,
you have your external signals.

00:45:30.030 --> 00:45:35.550
What you can do is to have an
extra step in your transformer

00:45:35.550 --> 00:45:42.470
block, where what you do is
letting the tokens representing

00:45:42.470 --> 00:45:48.670
the image latents interact
with the time step interacts

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:54.150
with the label embedding
via cross-attention.

00:45:54.150 --> 00:45:57.190
So here you can think of
it as you have queries

00:45:57.190 --> 00:45:59.870
which are your noise latents.

00:45:59.870 --> 00:46:03.630
And you're wondering which
ones of your conditions

00:46:03.630 --> 00:46:09.270
are relevant for
your noise patch.

00:46:09.270 --> 00:46:12.670
And then you do a
query times key,

00:46:12.670 --> 00:46:15.490
let's say a dot
product of those,

00:46:15.490 --> 00:46:17.050
and then you multiply by value.

00:46:17.050 --> 00:46:21.750
So you express
your noise latents

00:46:21.750 --> 00:46:26.030
as a function of
your conditions.

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:27.480
So this can be a second option.

00:46:27.480 --> 00:46:31.700
And the third option is
to just say, OK, actually

00:46:31.700 --> 00:46:34.060
we're going to
take these vectors,

00:46:34.060 --> 00:46:35.900
and we're just
going to concatenate

00:46:35.900 --> 00:46:38.860
that in the input sequence.

00:46:38.860 --> 00:46:40.580
Very easy.

00:46:40.580 --> 00:46:45.580
Well, it turns out that after
they did all these experiments,

00:46:45.580 --> 00:46:52.340
they saw that the adaptive layer
norm was the most performant.

00:46:52.340 --> 00:46:54.420
I'm going to give you
a preview of what we're

00:46:54.420 --> 00:46:59.060
going to see in lecture 7,
which is how to quantify

00:46:59.060 --> 00:47:01.180
how good an image is.

00:47:01.180 --> 00:47:05.340
So there is a metric called
the Fréchet Inception Distance,

00:47:05.340 --> 00:47:09.700
the FID, which is the one
that is represented here

00:47:09.700 --> 00:47:14.300
where the lower that
metric, the better it is.

00:47:14.300 --> 00:47:19.660
And what you see here is when
you use adaptive layer norm,

00:47:19.660 --> 00:47:24.260
you get the bottom
curve that allows

00:47:24.260 --> 00:47:27.940
you to get better quality
image quality compared

00:47:27.940 --> 00:47:32.340
to the other ones as a
function of how far you

00:47:32.340 --> 00:47:35.427
are in the training process.

00:47:35.427 --> 00:47:37.260
So if you saw, there's
actually two versions

00:47:37.260 --> 00:47:38.800
of the adaptive layer norm.

00:47:38.800 --> 00:47:41.620
There is adaptive layer norm,
and there's adaptive layer norm

00:47:41.620 --> 00:47:42.980
0.

00:47:42.980 --> 00:47:47.340
I'm going to tell you
what that 0 is about.

00:47:47.340 --> 00:47:51.620
But let's focus on
adaptive layer norm.

00:47:51.620 --> 00:47:55.540
So I could very well
just give you the formula

00:47:55.540 --> 00:47:58.760
and tell you that it
is adaptive layer norm.

00:47:58.760 --> 00:48:03.620
But I think it's very
convenient to just build

00:48:03.620 --> 00:48:08.060
an intuition as to
why it may make sense.

00:48:08.060 --> 00:48:13.900
So I'm going to use
an example for that.

00:48:13.900 --> 00:48:20.500
So I mentioned each patch can
be represented by a vector.

00:48:20.500 --> 00:48:27.290
Let's assume that we can
interpret this vector

00:48:27.290 --> 00:48:29.850
and say that let's
say a dimension is

00:48:29.850 --> 00:48:32.410
responsible for something
and another one for something

00:48:32.410 --> 00:48:34.410
else, which may not be true.

00:48:34.410 --> 00:48:35.910
But for the sake
of this example,

00:48:35.910 --> 00:48:38.690
let's suppose it is true.

00:48:38.690 --> 00:48:40.570
So remember, we are
in the latent space,

00:48:40.570 --> 00:48:44.610
and we can have some
semantic interpretation

00:48:44.610 --> 00:48:45.710
of the dimensions.

00:48:45.710 --> 00:48:48.730
So there is some truth
to what I'm saying.

00:48:48.730 --> 00:48:53.890
But let's suppose you have a
vector where let's say the 10th

00:48:53.890 --> 00:48:58.150
dimension represents the
intensity of, let's say,

00:48:58.150 --> 00:49:00.490
the brown color.

00:49:00.490 --> 00:49:04.650
Let's say dimension 24 is
how round the shape is.

00:49:04.650 --> 00:49:08.770
Dimension 59 is how
fluffy the local details

00:49:08.770 --> 00:49:14.170
are and then dimension 130,
which is how white the color is.

00:49:14.170 --> 00:49:18.290
So let's suppose you
have such dimensions.

00:49:18.290 --> 00:49:23.290
And now let's suppose
you want to generate

00:49:23.290 --> 00:49:30.210
an image with the condition,
a brown fluffy teddy bear.

00:49:30.210 --> 00:49:33.250
So you want to
generate that image.

00:49:33.250 --> 00:49:36.630
And as you remember, generating
an image is not one shot.

00:49:36.630 --> 00:49:39.010
Typically you do
that iteratively

00:49:39.010 --> 00:49:41.890
where you take a noisy
latent as inputs.

00:49:41.890 --> 00:49:43.270
And then you have the condition.

00:49:43.270 --> 00:49:45.070
You have how noisy
that image is,

00:49:45.070 --> 00:49:48.170
and you try to have something
as output that will allow

00:49:48.170 --> 00:49:51.690
you to denoise your image.

00:49:51.690 --> 00:49:55.570
And let's suppose that
we're at a stage where

00:49:55.570 --> 00:49:57.690
there is a lot of noise.

00:49:57.690 --> 00:50:00.810
So, the noise can be
represented on a scale that's

00:50:00.810 --> 00:50:03.730
here we're going to
assume is between 0 and 1,

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:05.150
where 0 is super noisy.

00:50:05.150 --> 00:50:07.850
1 is clean.

00:50:07.850 --> 00:50:14.170
And let's assume that time here
is 0.1-- so a lot of noise.

00:50:14.170 --> 00:50:20.400
So here what you would
want is to focus on-- so

00:50:20.400 --> 00:50:22.760
at that stage of
the generation, you

00:50:22.760 --> 00:50:27.960
want to focus on surfacing the
global structure of the image

00:50:27.960 --> 00:50:29.300
because it's still super noisy.

00:50:29.300 --> 00:50:33.320
You're just trying to sketch
what the final image is.

00:50:33.320 --> 00:50:37.660
So here you want some
brownness in the image.

00:50:37.660 --> 00:50:40.080
You want some round
shapes that may represent

00:50:40.080 --> 00:50:41.180
your teddy bear and so on.

00:50:41.180 --> 00:50:45.040
So you want these
features to surface.

00:50:45.040 --> 00:50:49.920
So our intuition tells us
that you want these dimensions

00:50:49.920 --> 00:50:54.760
to have a higher intensity.

00:50:54.760 --> 00:50:57.000
In contrast to
that, you don't want

00:50:57.000 --> 00:51:00.540
to have local details just
yet because it's very noisy.

00:51:00.540 --> 00:51:02.180
So that's not the time.

00:51:02.180 --> 00:51:04.120
And then you don't
want any white color

00:51:04.120 --> 00:51:05.980
because your teddy
bear is brown.

00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:08.840
It's not white.

00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:12.500
Now let's suppose you have
the same condition as inputs,

00:51:12.500 --> 00:51:14.720
but now you're at a
more advanced stage

00:51:14.720 --> 00:51:17.400
in your generation process.

00:51:17.400 --> 00:51:23.720
Now t equals 0.9, which
corresponds to almost no noise

00:51:23.720 --> 00:51:25.920
in your input image.

00:51:25.920 --> 00:51:29.080
So here you may
wonder, OK, so now

00:51:29.080 --> 00:51:32.360
the input is already
quite figured out

00:51:32.360 --> 00:51:33.860
in terms of global structure.

00:51:33.860 --> 00:51:38.080
Now I need to focus
on the details.

00:51:38.080 --> 00:51:41.960
So you want the, let's
say fluffy details,

00:51:41.960 --> 00:51:45.080
to have a higher intensity.

00:51:45.080 --> 00:51:47.200
And you don't want
the round shape

00:51:47.200 --> 00:51:51.200
to be that intense because
it's already there.

00:51:51.200 --> 00:51:53.140
And the brown color is same.

00:51:53.140 --> 00:51:55.140
It's already there and
then white color again.

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:58.440
You don't want it.

00:51:58.440 --> 00:52:03.960
So what I just
told you is how we

00:52:03.960 --> 00:52:09.720
would like different dimensions
of the patch embedding

00:52:09.720 --> 00:52:13.400
to have their
intensity modulated

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:16.370
as a function of the
condition, which is,

00:52:16.370 --> 00:52:20.910
for instance, the input
text and the noise

00:52:20.910 --> 00:52:22.490
level in which you are.

00:52:25.070 --> 00:52:33.550
So in that sense, what you want
is for your patch embedding

00:52:33.550 --> 00:52:38.270
to exhibit higher intensities
in the dimensions you care

00:52:38.270 --> 00:52:44.230
and lower intensity in the
dimensions you don't care.

00:52:44.230 --> 00:52:48.790
So in that sense,
what I propose is how

00:52:48.790 --> 00:52:50.890
about we modulate these tokens?

00:52:50.890 --> 00:52:53.710
How about we modulate
these vectors?

00:52:53.710 --> 00:52:58.990
So here what we would do
is to take the condition

00:52:58.990 --> 00:53:05.250
to take the time step as input
and to obtain some vectors.

00:53:05.250 --> 00:53:08.370
So let's call them
alpha, gamma, and beta.

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:10.270
So let's assume we
obtain these vectors that

00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:15.310
are a function of the
condition and the noise level.

00:53:15.310 --> 00:53:18.950
And what we want to
do is to modulate

00:53:18.950 --> 00:53:21.190
the embedding of the patch.

00:53:21.190 --> 00:53:23.790
So how would we do that?

00:53:23.790 --> 00:53:29.510
Well, you take your vector.

00:53:29.510 --> 00:53:33.590
You do some normalization
just because it's not

00:53:33.590 --> 00:53:35.130
part of the
modulation procedure,

00:53:35.130 --> 00:53:39.190
but it's just good because it
just improves your training

00:53:39.190 --> 00:53:39.850
stability.

00:53:39.850 --> 00:53:44.110
So let's suppose you
normalize your vector.

00:53:44.110 --> 00:53:48.750
And you do an element-wise
multiplication

00:53:48.750 --> 00:53:53.070
with 1 plus gamma.

00:53:53.070 --> 00:53:56.550
So if gamma is equal
to the vector 0,

00:53:56.550 --> 00:53:59.150
the element-wise
multiplication of this and one

00:53:59.150 --> 00:54:04.750
is completely [INAUDIBLE]
and nothing changes.

00:54:04.750 --> 00:54:08.790
But if gamma is a
vector that tells you

00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:11.500
that, OK, a given
dimension should be higher

00:54:11.500 --> 00:54:14.260
and another dimension
should be lower,

00:54:14.260 --> 00:54:19.900
then it will scale your x.

00:54:19.900 --> 00:54:24.700
And by the way, I said
1, but it's the vector 1.

00:54:24.700 --> 00:54:28.580
So you can think of it as a
vector matching the dimension

00:54:28.580 --> 00:54:32.700
of gamma of just filled with 1.

00:54:32.700 --> 00:54:34.980
So in that sense, you
can think of gamma

00:54:34.980 --> 00:54:39.060
as being a scale
coefficient, a scale

00:54:39.060 --> 00:54:42.040
vector because it scales x.

00:54:44.700 --> 00:54:50.060
Then you can add
beta, which is a bias,

00:54:50.060 --> 00:54:54.660
and perform some
operation, and have

00:54:54.660 --> 00:54:58.180
an element-wise multiplication
of this whole quantity

00:54:58.180 --> 00:55:00.500
with alpha.

00:55:00.500 --> 00:55:04.020
And alpha will be your gate.

00:55:04.020 --> 00:55:08.060
Because what you do
is you take your x.

00:55:08.060 --> 00:55:09.370
You flow the same x.

00:55:09.370 --> 00:55:13.770
And what you do is you make
some operation with that x,

00:55:13.770 --> 00:55:15.190
and you scale it.

00:55:15.190 --> 00:55:19.250
You shift it, and you
gate it with alpha.

00:55:19.250 --> 00:55:25.010
And alpha is there to
tell you how much of that

00:55:25.010 --> 00:55:30.010
change is going to
flow in your embedding.

00:55:30.010 --> 00:55:34.050
So in that sense, I'm just
going to, again, stress

00:55:34.050 --> 00:55:39.650
that these stars are
element-wise multiplications.

00:55:39.650 --> 00:55:43.790
And alpha, gamma, and beta
have very specific roles.

00:55:43.790 --> 00:55:47.290
So alpha really gate
keeps this whole quantity

00:55:47.290 --> 00:55:49.810
from being injected.

00:55:49.810 --> 00:55:54.430
Gamma is responsible for
scaling the quantity.

00:55:54.430 --> 00:55:56.650
And then beta is
there to shift it

00:55:56.650 --> 00:56:02.370
because it's a plus
with that quantity.

00:56:02.370 --> 00:56:04.530
And do you remember
I told you I'm

00:56:04.530 --> 00:56:09.650
going to say what
adaptive layer norm 0 is?

00:56:09.650 --> 00:56:11.850
Do you remember in the chart?

00:56:11.850 --> 00:56:19.410
Well, adaptive layer norm
0 is adaptive layer norm

00:56:19.410 --> 00:56:22.730
where when you
start training, you

00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:29.110
initialize your gates to 0, such
that there is no modification.

00:56:29.110 --> 00:56:32.710
So by the way you, you set
alpha and gamma and beta to 0,

00:56:32.710 --> 00:56:34.390
I believe in most
implementations,

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:37.690
but alpha being to
0 already guarantees

00:56:37.690 --> 00:56:43.430
that at the very beginning
of training, you--

00:56:43.430 --> 00:56:43.930
yeah.

00:56:43.930 --> 00:56:45.310
So the question
is unconditional.

00:56:45.310 --> 00:56:45.810
Yes.

00:56:45.810 --> 00:56:47.268
So at the very
beginning, you don't

00:56:47.268 --> 00:56:51.910
have any impact from the
condition on your embedding.

00:56:54.690 --> 00:56:55.630
So far so good?

00:56:58.930 --> 00:57:02.030
So we will have a detailed
example after that.

00:57:02.030 --> 00:57:04.350
So I hope it will be
also clearer there.

00:57:04.350 --> 00:57:08.960
But that is the
high level intuition

00:57:08.960 --> 00:57:14.800
behind why adaptive
LayerNorm is useful.

00:57:14.800 --> 00:57:20.720
It is there to highlight
dimensions that are

00:57:20.720 --> 00:57:24.320
important for the task at hand.

00:57:24.320 --> 00:57:28.680
And so here what we do is-- so
just recapping what we said.

00:57:28.680 --> 00:57:30.340
We take our condition.

00:57:30.340 --> 00:57:31.540
So it's the time step.

00:57:31.540 --> 00:57:33.840
It's the class
label-- so t and c.

00:57:33.840 --> 00:57:35.920
We add them
together, and then we

00:57:35.920 --> 00:57:40.040
use just a multi-layer
perceptron, which is just

00:57:40.040 --> 00:57:42.200
a linear projection,
some activation,

00:57:42.200 --> 00:57:47.240
and another linear projection,
to obtain this gate, scale,

00:57:47.240 --> 00:57:51.320
and shift alpha,
gamma and beta, that

00:57:51.320 --> 00:57:58.080
are then used to modulate
the patch embeddings.

00:57:58.080 --> 00:58:00.280
So the question is,
is t corresponding

00:58:00.280 --> 00:58:01.580
to the diffusion time step?

00:58:01.580 --> 00:58:02.480
Yes.

00:58:02.480 --> 00:58:07.020
So it's how noisy you are
in your generation process.

00:58:07.020 --> 00:58:09.420
So one example is in the
very, very beginning.

00:58:09.420 --> 00:58:12.080
If let's say, your
input is super noisy,

00:58:12.080 --> 00:58:16.480
then t is going to be very
close to 0, for instance.

00:58:16.480 --> 00:58:20.200
But you will represent that by
your vector that is composed

00:58:20.200 --> 00:58:23.320
of sinusoidal functions.

00:58:23.320 --> 00:58:24.340
Yeah, that's right.

00:58:24.340 --> 00:58:27.220
So the question is, is
this a reverse process?

00:58:27.220 --> 00:58:28.920
Well, actually even
during training,

00:58:28.920 --> 00:58:33.560
you would just inject the t
that corresponds to the noise

00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:35.920
level of your image.

00:58:35.920 --> 00:58:38.580
So we're going to see
exactly an example of this.

00:58:38.580 --> 00:58:40.440
So I'll actually
answer your question

00:58:40.440 --> 00:58:42.660
in great detail in a second.

00:58:45.240 --> 00:58:46.360
Perfect.

00:58:46.360 --> 00:58:51.600
Well, with that, what I
propose is we wrap up this,

00:58:51.600 --> 00:58:53.760
and I walk you through
an end to end example

00:58:53.760 --> 00:58:55.440
of what that looks like.

00:58:55.440 --> 00:59:01.270
So your patch embeddings, you
run them through your diffusion

00:59:01.270 --> 00:59:03.030
transformer blocks.

00:59:03.030 --> 00:59:05.230
There can be several of them.

00:59:05.230 --> 00:59:11.270
And at the very end, you
obtain encoded tokens-- so

00:59:11.270 --> 00:59:13.630
patch-embedding tokens that
are aware of one another

00:59:13.630 --> 00:59:16.590
because they went through
all these operations.

00:59:16.590 --> 00:59:20.470
And remember what
we want is to have

00:59:20.470 --> 00:59:23.350
an output that is of the
same shape as the input.

00:59:23.350 --> 00:59:29.550
And so here the step is to
reshape that output in the shape

00:59:29.550 --> 00:59:32.110
that matters to you.

00:59:32.110 --> 00:59:38.830
Well, the authors of DiT applied
this to the DDP end use case,

00:59:38.830 --> 00:59:43.190
and they actually assumed that
they were not just predicting

00:59:43.190 --> 00:59:46.630
the mean but also the
covariance matrix.

00:59:46.630 --> 00:59:48.970
So that's not very
common by the way.

00:59:48.970 --> 00:59:53.190
So you would more predict
the mean but not both.

00:59:53.190 --> 00:59:57.030
But I'm saying this
because that just explains

00:59:57.030 --> 00:59:59.630
why there are two outputs.

00:59:59.630 --> 01:00:02.030
But in our case, we would
just have one output--

01:00:02.030 --> 01:00:04.790
in our case, the
velocity, which should be

01:00:04.790 --> 01:00:09.870
in the same shape as the input.

01:00:09.870 --> 01:00:18.150
And what the authors saw
was that if you take the DiT

01:00:18.150 --> 01:00:22.270
architecture and if
you scale it in terms

01:00:22.270 --> 01:00:26.270
of number of
parameters, then you

01:00:26.270 --> 01:00:29.990
get better and better results.

01:00:29.990 --> 01:00:34.050
And they scaled it in terms
of number of parameters,

01:00:34.050 --> 01:00:37.350
but they also scaled it
in terms of how granular

01:00:37.350 --> 01:00:42.030
the patches were, because the
patches they have dimension

01:00:42.030 --> 01:00:44.910
p times p times c.

01:00:44.910 --> 01:00:50.070
And if you take big patches,
then you have a much coarser

01:00:50.070 --> 01:00:54.950
view than let's say, a
small patch, small p.

01:00:54.950 --> 01:00:58.180
The problem with the
number of parameters

01:00:58.180 --> 01:01:01.665
is it does not convey
that dimension.

01:01:01.665 --> 01:01:03.540
So you can have the same
number of parameters

01:01:03.540 --> 01:01:11.460
but vary p, which is why
the authors also use FLOPs.

01:01:11.460 --> 01:01:14.620
So who has heard
of FLOPs before?

01:01:14.620 --> 01:01:16.500
Floating Point
Operations is just

01:01:16.500 --> 01:01:18.540
a measure of how many
operations you're

01:01:18.540 --> 01:01:20.340
doing in your forward pass.

01:01:20.340 --> 01:01:24.780
And that actually includes patch
size, because if you remember,

01:01:24.780 --> 01:01:30.648
your input has a length
of I over P squared.

01:01:30.648 --> 01:01:32.940
So if you have a smaller p,
you have a bigger sequence,

01:01:32.940 --> 01:01:36.000
which is reflected in
your flops measure.

01:01:38.540 --> 01:01:42.640
And in particular, so
that's pretty interesting.

01:01:42.640 --> 01:01:46.440
So you should not only
scale one and not the other.

01:01:46.440 --> 01:01:51.220
So they see that if they
scale the transformer size,

01:01:51.220 --> 01:01:54.020
they get better
results but not great.

01:01:54.020 --> 01:01:57.260
Now, if they only
scale the patch size,

01:01:57.260 --> 01:01:59.620
they get better
results but not great.

01:01:59.620 --> 01:02:04.940
But if they scale both the
transformer size and the patch

01:02:04.940 --> 01:02:11.380
size, then they get
amazing results.

01:02:11.380 --> 01:02:13.960
That's what you see in
the figure on the right

01:02:13.960 --> 01:02:17.620
and also the chart
that tells you this

01:02:17.620 --> 01:02:20.440
as a function of FID,
which as I mentioned,

01:02:20.440 --> 01:02:23.740
is a metric we will
see in two lectures,

01:02:23.740 --> 01:02:29.060
where the lower the
value, the better it is.

01:02:29.060 --> 01:02:33.420
And that was the DiT.

01:02:33.420 --> 01:02:37.420
Given that the DiT is a pretty
important architecture, what

01:02:37.420 --> 01:02:39.980
we will do is take
our time to go

01:02:39.980 --> 01:02:44.900
through an end to end example
of exactly how that works.

01:02:44.900 --> 01:02:51.460
So our goal is to take an
input-- so here, the input text,

01:02:51.460 --> 01:02:55.050
generate an image of a
teddy bear that is reading,

01:02:55.050 --> 01:02:58.010
and what we want as an
output is the image itself

01:02:58.010 --> 01:03:01.730
or the generated
image, I should say.

01:03:01.730 --> 01:03:07.410
So in lecture 4, we saw that
we have a lot of incentive

01:03:07.410 --> 01:03:09.430
to do all of this
in the latent space,

01:03:09.430 --> 01:03:11.450
because it's more
tractable computationally,

01:03:11.450 --> 01:03:14.290
and it's just
easier for a model.

01:03:14.290 --> 01:03:17.330
So what we'll do is
we'll take a look

01:03:17.330 --> 01:03:23.970
at the latent space learned by
a VAE that is at our disposal.

01:03:23.970 --> 01:03:26.150
And you see you have
two distributions.

01:03:26.150 --> 01:03:29.290
You have on the bottom left,
the initial distribution, which

01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:32.530
is very easy to sample from, and
the target distribution, which

01:03:32.530 --> 01:03:36.730
is the one that you
want to sample from.

01:03:36.730 --> 01:03:39.885
So what you do is you sample
from your easy distribution,

01:03:39.885 --> 01:03:42.010
typically your Gaussian
distribution in your latent

01:03:42.010 --> 01:03:42.550
space.

01:03:42.550 --> 01:03:44.970
Let's call it z 0.

01:03:44.970 --> 01:03:48.010
And you obtain this thing--

01:03:48.010 --> 01:03:51.610
dimension I times I times C.

01:03:51.610 --> 01:03:54.890
What you do is you
take that image,

01:03:54.890 --> 01:03:58.770
and you divide it into patches.

01:03:58.770 --> 01:04:03.930
So each element has a
dimension of p times p times c.

01:04:03.930 --> 01:04:05.890
So if you do some
counting, you will

01:04:05.890 --> 01:04:10.770
see that I squared
over p squared patches.

01:04:10.770 --> 01:04:17.050
And this is the length
of your sequence.

01:04:17.050 --> 01:04:21.090
So you take each patch, and
you want to represent them

01:04:21.090 --> 01:04:23.130
with a vector.

01:04:23.130 --> 01:04:25.910
So you just perform
a linear projection.

01:04:25.910 --> 01:04:28.130
And you obtain a
vector of, let's say,

01:04:28.130 --> 01:04:32.750
dimension D. Great
for the patches.

01:04:32.750 --> 01:04:36.210
Now let's take care
of the conditions.

01:04:36.210 --> 01:04:37.990
So you have the
timestamp embedding,

01:04:37.990 --> 01:04:40.210
and then you have the label
embedding corresponding

01:04:40.210 --> 01:04:43.810
to let's say the teddy bear.

01:04:43.810 --> 01:04:45.150
And what you do is super simple.

01:04:45.150 --> 01:04:46.250
You just add them.

01:04:46.250 --> 01:04:47.930
Add them together.

01:04:47.930 --> 01:04:52.000
And you obtain your
condition embedding.

01:04:52.000 --> 01:04:55.080
So far so good?

01:04:55.080 --> 01:04:59.280
So here we are with
our patchified input,

01:04:59.280 --> 01:05:05.920
where each element is
represented with an embedding.

01:05:05.920 --> 01:05:07.772
There's something I
have not told you about.

01:05:07.772 --> 01:05:09.480
And it's actually
something that Shervine

01:05:09.480 --> 01:05:15.240
will cover in great detail
in hopefully 20 minutes.

01:05:15.240 --> 01:05:19.400
When you perform this
self-attention operations,

01:05:19.400 --> 01:05:23.280
the great thing is you let
everyone interact with everyone,

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:28.520
but then you lose the concept of
how far different elements are

01:05:28.520 --> 01:05:31.720
just because you have
direct links, which

01:05:31.720 --> 01:05:37.500
is why you need to specify in
which position each input is.

01:05:37.500 --> 01:05:40.720
You need to have
a way to do that.

01:05:40.720 --> 01:05:42.440
And here a very
naive approach is

01:05:42.440 --> 01:05:47.400
to just add some embedding
that represents the position.

01:05:47.400 --> 01:05:49.380
This is not the norm nowadays.

01:05:49.380 --> 01:05:51.860
Shervine will go
deeper in there,

01:05:51.860 --> 01:05:55.800
but let's assume here that
we add a position embedding

01:05:55.800 --> 01:05:57.360
to the patch embedding.

01:05:57.360 --> 01:06:02.560
And here we obtain a
position-aware patch embedding.

01:06:02.560 --> 01:06:06.560
And we do the same for all
the patches in our input.

01:06:06.560 --> 01:06:10.940
And nowadays, GPUs
they love matrices.

01:06:10.940 --> 01:06:13.420
So we just put everything
in a matrix format.

01:06:13.420 --> 01:06:16.080
So here we concatenate
everything.

01:06:16.080 --> 01:06:20.000
And we also have our
conditioned embedding vector.

01:06:20.000 --> 01:06:24.120
Well, let's start with that.

01:06:24.120 --> 01:06:28.560
So I mentioned that the
diffusion transformer, so one

01:06:28.560 --> 01:06:30.480
method of injecting
the conditions

01:06:30.480 --> 01:06:36.560
is to modulate the patch
embeddings with the conditions.

01:06:36.560 --> 01:06:42.160
So a first step here is to
obtain the gate, the scale,

01:06:42.160 --> 01:06:46.710
and the shift coefficients
corresponding to that condition.

01:06:46.710 --> 01:06:47.870
So let's do that here.

01:06:47.870 --> 01:06:51.830
We input this embedding
through an MLP layer, which

01:06:51.830 --> 01:06:54.510
is a projection
and an activation

01:06:54.510 --> 01:06:57.110
function to introduce
nonlinearities, and then

01:06:57.110 --> 01:06:59.110
another projection.

01:06:59.110 --> 01:07:02.390
And we obtain six quantities--

01:07:02.390 --> 01:07:06.030
two for gate, two for
scale, two for shifts.

01:07:06.030 --> 01:07:06.630
So why two?

01:07:06.630 --> 01:07:11.190
Because we will have two
operations in the diffusion

01:07:11.190 --> 01:07:12.030
transformer block.

01:07:12.030 --> 01:07:13.970
One is the
self-attention operation.

01:07:13.970 --> 01:07:17.090
The other one is the
feed-forward neural network.

01:07:17.090 --> 01:07:22.430
And we want these quantities
to be respectively

01:07:22.430 --> 01:07:25.830
to go to their own operation.

01:07:25.830 --> 01:07:33.470
So let's go back to our position
where patch-embedding matrix.

01:07:33.470 --> 01:07:35.770
We input that into our block.

01:07:35.770 --> 01:07:40.030
So what we do is we perform a
layer normalization operation.

01:07:40.030 --> 01:07:44.820
And right after that, we
use gamma 1 and beta 1

01:07:44.820 --> 01:07:48.300
to scale and shift them.

01:07:48.300 --> 01:07:51.580
We input them to the
self-attention layer,

01:07:51.580 --> 01:07:57.540
and then we get the
result with alpha 1,

01:07:57.540 --> 01:08:03.700
which we add to the input.

01:08:03.700 --> 01:08:06.320
So this is becoming very
complicated on the slide.

01:08:06.320 --> 01:08:08.300
So I'm just going to
represent this operation

01:08:08.300 --> 01:08:10.460
with just self-attention
and then alpha

01:08:10.460 --> 01:08:13.120
1, gamma 1, and beta 1.

01:08:16.180 --> 01:08:21.420
And as you remember,
this is what happens.

01:08:21.420 --> 01:08:23.340
So you take your
input, and then you

01:08:23.340 --> 01:08:27.760
do the attention operation with
the scaled and shifted inputs.

01:08:27.760 --> 01:08:31.140
And you get it by alpha 1, where
the star operation here is just

01:08:31.140 --> 01:08:34.979
the element-wise operation.

01:08:34.979 --> 01:08:39.220
You do the same thing for the
feed forward neural network,

01:08:39.220 --> 01:08:45.380
along with the alpha 2,
gamma 2, beta 2 coefficients.

01:08:45.380 --> 01:08:54.819
And you obtain an output matrix
that takes into consideration

01:08:54.819 --> 01:08:57.279
all other patch embeddings.

01:08:57.279 --> 01:08:59.620
So it's something that went
through this self-attention

01:08:59.620 --> 01:09:01.260
computation.

01:09:01.260 --> 01:09:06.939
So that's why I'm saying
it's contextualized

01:09:06.939 --> 01:09:11.500
because it's in the context
of the other patch embeddings.

01:09:11.500 --> 01:09:15.660
But now what you want is
to have a prediction that

01:09:15.660 --> 01:09:17.700
matches the input shape.

01:09:17.700 --> 01:09:21.560
So what you do is you
perform some normalization,

01:09:21.560 --> 01:09:25.300
some linear operation
reshaping in order

01:09:25.300 --> 01:09:28.740
to have your
prediction, which is

01:09:28.740 --> 01:09:32.260
the predicted
velocity of your input

01:09:32.260 --> 01:09:37.420
z at time t with condition c.

01:09:37.420 --> 01:09:43.850
In the very beginning,
z is z, 0, t is 0,

01:09:43.850 --> 01:09:46.890
c is, well, just
your class label.

01:09:46.890 --> 01:09:50.990
Just I want an image of a teddy
bear that is reading a book.

01:09:53.609 --> 01:09:54.590
So you have this.

01:09:54.590 --> 01:09:56.630
So just recapping
exactly what I said.

01:09:56.630 --> 01:09:58.390
So we have the latent z, 0.

01:09:58.390 --> 01:10:00.070
We have the time
step t equals 0.

01:10:00.070 --> 01:10:05.090
We have the class label all fed
into this diffusion transformer.

01:10:05.090 --> 01:10:10.450
And at the end of it, we get
the predicted velocity at z,

01:10:10.450 --> 01:10:15.330
0 time t equals 0
and class label C.

01:10:15.330 --> 01:10:17.130
And that's great.

01:10:17.130 --> 01:10:20.970
But we're not done here.

01:10:20.970 --> 01:10:24.970
We're only done here for
the iteration that allows us

01:10:24.970 --> 01:10:28.970
to go to step 0 plus delta t.

01:10:28.970 --> 01:10:33.170
And we perform-- so the
formula that I wrote

01:10:33.170 --> 01:10:35.210
is just a reference
to the formula

01:10:35.210 --> 01:10:37.650
that we derived in
the flow-matching case

01:10:37.650 --> 01:10:42.150
where you're just
numerically solving.

01:10:42.150 --> 01:10:47.130
So here's an Euler
solver that just

01:10:47.130 --> 01:10:51.970
allows you to go from initial
to target by just following

01:10:51.970 --> 01:10:52.990
the vector field.

01:10:52.990 --> 01:10:55.050
So that's exactly
what you're doing.

01:10:55.050 --> 01:11:00.690
So you obtain this
z 0 plus delta t

01:11:00.690 --> 01:11:04.450
that you can also
have represented

01:11:04.450 --> 01:11:07.470
like this on the left
in your latent space.

01:11:07.470 --> 01:11:08.730
So that's great.

01:11:08.730 --> 01:11:11.010
So now let's do one more.

01:11:11.010 --> 01:11:13.690
You're at time step t i.

01:11:13.690 --> 01:11:19.410
So you input your latent z t
i, along with the time step t i

01:11:19.410 --> 01:11:21.730
and the condition
c that is not--

01:11:21.730 --> 01:11:24.810
or the class label c
that is not changing--

01:11:24.810 --> 01:11:28.195
so your input text.

01:11:28.195 --> 01:11:30.070
So you put that in the
diffusion transformer.

01:11:30.070 --> 01:11:35.533
Again, you get a predicted
velocity for that latent z

01:11:35.533 --> 01:11:40.240
t i for that time step t i
and for the class label c.

01:11:40.240 --> 01:11:41.740
And you go through
the same thing.

01:11:41.740 --> 01:11:43.900
So you have the prediction.

01:11:43.900 --> 01:11:48.480
And you obtain z t
i plus 1 by, again,

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:51.840
following the Euler method.

01:11:51.840 --> 01:11:56.440
So here on the left side,
you have the next step.

01:11:56.440 --> 01:12:01.720
You continue again and again
and again and again and again up

01:12:01.720 --> 01:12:04.040
until z 1.

01:12:04.040 --> 01:12:05.740
So now you're at t equal 1.

01:12:05.740 --> 01:12:06.340
You have z 1.

01:12:06.340 --> 01:12:07.640
That's great.

01:12:07.640 --> 01:12:10.420
Well, what do you have?

01:12:10.420 --> 01:12:10.920
Well, no.

01:12:10.920 --> 01:12:11.820
It's not noise.

01:12:11.820 --> 01:12:14.060
It's just it's the latent space.

01:12:14.060 --> 01:12:18.620
So from a human perspective,
you don't know what that is.

01:12:18.620 --> 01:12:23.320
So that's why I represent
it with the noise image.

01:12:23.320 --> 01:12:27.000
But it is actually
your final latent.

01:12:27.000 --> 01:12:34.160
And so the last step is to
take the final latent Z1

01:12:34.160 --> 01:12:37.502
and pass it through
your VA decoder,

01:12:37.502 --> 01:12:39.460
because you want to go
back to the pixel space,

01:12:39.460 --> 01:12:43.260
because we humans,
we understand pixels.

01:12:43.260 --> 01:12:46.960
We don't understand the
latent, or at least not yet.

01:12:46.960 --> 01:12:50.000
And we get the
pixel space image,

01:12:50.000 --> 01:12:55.520
which is the image of your
teddy bear reading a book.

01:12:55.520 --> 01:12:57.200
And that's great.

01:12:57.200 --> 01:12:59.480
And you will tell me,
Afshine, that's great.

01:12:59.480 --> 01:13:01.280
We solved it.

01:13:01.280 --> 01:13:07.320
Well, the truth is, these
days people are actually

01:13:07.320 --> 01:13:09.520
using a variation
of the diffusion

01:13:09.520 --> 01:13:12.520
transformer for a
number of reasons

01:13:12.520 --> 01:13:16.320
that we're going to see now.

01:13:16.320 --> 01:13:21.400
And again, I just want to repeat
one thing I said earlier, which

01:13:21.400 --> 01:13:26.040
is there is a number, infinite
number of architectures

01:13:26.040 --> 01:13:30.800
out there, infinite number
of variations, combinations.

01:13:30.800 --> 01:13:36.190
My hope is to just motivate
why some elements that we're

01:13:36.190 --> 01:13:40.390
going to see are being
reused actively these days,

01:13:40.390 --> 01:13:45.270
but they're by no
means exhaustive.

01:13:45.270 --> 01:13:52.390
So let's go back to the example
that we had with respect to us

01:13:52.390 --> 01:13:56.470
wanting to modulate
patch embeddings.

01:13:56.470 --> 01:13:58.790
So do you remember
the example we

01:13:58.790 --> 01:14:03.910
used where we said we want
a brown fluffy teddy bear

01:14:03.910 --> 01:14:06.070
and we had all these dimensions?

01:14:06.070 --> 01:14:09.270
And we said, let's
say at t equals 0.1,

01:14:09.270 --> 01:14:13.252
we wanted brown color and
round shape to be highlighted

01:14:13.252 --> 01:14:14.210
but not the other ones.

01:14:14.210 --> 01:14:16.990
Do you remember that?

01:14:16.990 --> 01:14:19.670
So let's suppose now
I'm changing the class

01:14:19.670 --> 01:14:23.390
label or the condition,
the text input,

01:14:23.390 --> 01:14:29.150
to brown fluffy teddy bear
surrounded by white walls.

01:14:29.150 --> 01:14:31.910
So imagine I do that.

01:14:31.910 --> 01:14:35.630
Then the white
color dimension, you

01:14:35.630 --> 01:14:38.710
would want to also have
it emphasized because you

01:14:38.710 --> 01:14:43.670
have white in your input.

01:14:43.670 --> 01:14:48.310
But remember that
this modulation

01:14:48.310 --> 01:14:54.670
is happening for all tokens,
regardless of where they are.

01:14:54.670 --> 01:14:56.590
So in other words,
what we're saying

01:14:56.590 --> 01:15:02.150
is if you want to predict the
velocity to denoise an image,

01:15:02.150 --> 01:15:06.150
you're going to modulate
all patch embeddings

01:15:06.150 --> 01:15:08.950
in the same way.

01:15:08.950 --> 01:15:12.590
But here what you want is for
places where you have a Teddy

01:15:12.590 --> 01:15:15.050
bear to have Brown
that's increased

01:15:15.050 --> 01:15:18.270
but not the white
color and for places

01:15:18.270 --> 01:15:20.190
where there is the
white wall to have

01:15:20.190 --> 01:15:21.970
white increase but not brown.

01:15:24.510 --> 01:15:26.770
Well, you cannot do
that with the DiT,

01:15:26.770 --> 01:15:33.940
and that's a big limitation,
which is the reason why we want

01:15:33.940 --> 01:15:35.520
to change a little
bit our approach.

01:15:35.520 --> 01:15:36.187
So that's great.

01:15:36.187 --> 01:15:38.920
We're having the
self-attention mechanism there.

01:15:38.920 --> 01:15:40.220
It's amazing.

01:15:40.220 --> 01:15:44.500
The only thing we want to
do is to change the way

01:15:44.500 --> 01:15:48.500
we're injecting conditions.

01:15:48.500 --> 01:15:53.020
So modulating with
respect to the time step,

01:15:53.020 --> 01:15:55.700
I think we can all
agree that makes sense

01:15:55.700 --> 01:15:59.020
because all tokens
of your input,

01:15:59.020 --> 01:16:00.620
they're all at the
same time step.

01:16:00.620 --> 01:16:04.700
So it's very fair to
have the same treatment.

01:16:04.700 --> 01:16:09.140
But we need to do
something else when

01:16:09.140 --> 01:16:16.820
it comes to injecting the
text input from the user.

01:16:16.820 --> 01:16:22.940
We don't want to apply the
same treatment to all tokens.

01:16:22.940 --> 01:16:27.100
So out there, there
are two main classes

01:16:27.100 --> 01:16:34.780
of methods that allows us
to introduce some nuance

01:16:34.780 --> 01:16:36.820
to the way we do that.

01:16:36.820 --> 01:16:41.640
And while there is no standard
classification out there,

01:16:41.640 --> 01:16:44.540
I'm just telling you there is
two main classes of methods

01:16:44.540 --> 01:16:46.300
that seem to emerge--

01:16:46.300 --> 01:16:51.260
one that involves
cross-attention and one

01:16:51.260 --> 01:16:55.260
that involves joint attention.

01:16:55.260 --> 01:16:58.820
So before we go
further, I just want

01:16:58.820 --> 01:17:03.380
to explicit a little bit more
what these two correspond to.

01:17:03.380 --> 01:17:08.760
So cross-attention is the idea
of having something as input--

01:17:08.760 --> 01:17:11.580
so your queries, your
patch embeddings--

01:17:11.580 --> 01:17:17.340
and wondering how relevant your
keys and values, which here is

01:17:17.340 --> 01:17:21.080
your text, is to your queries.

01:17:23.650 --> 01:17:27.650
So in other words, I'm
going to use an analogy,

01:17:27.650 --> 01:17:30.610
and I hope it's something
that will be intuitive to you

01:17:30.610 --> 01:17:31.110
as well.

01:17:31.110 --> 01:17:35.490
So let's suppose you
have a painter that

01:17:35.490 --> 01:17:39.530
paints the final
image and then let's

01:17:39.530 --> 01:17:44.530
say a poet that writes
the instruction in a very

01:17:44.530 --> 01:17:47.410
eloquent way.

01:17:47.410 --> 01:17:52.010
So it's the same
thing as saying,

01:17:52.010 --> 01:17:55.850
if we were to say that the
painter, the one who determines

01:17:55.850 --> 01:17:58.770
what changes to
bring to the image,

01:17:58.770 --> 01:18:03.010
is actually looking at
a piece of text that

01:18:03.010 --> 01:18:08.770
is brought by the writer, and
just based on that determines

01:18:08.770 --> 01:18:10.470
what to change on the image.

01:18:10.470 --> 01:18:13.510
So it pays attention to the
input text for a given patch.

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:17.010
It says, OK, what
is the piece of text

01:18:17.010 --> 01:18:18.150
that is relevant to that?

01:18:18.150 --> 01:18:19.930
And then based on
that it says, OK, I

01:18:19.930 --> 01:18:21.870
need to change
this, change that.

01:18:21.870 --> 01:18:24.650
So that is cross-attention.

01:18:24.650 --> 01:18:27.490
So now what is joint attention?

01:18:27.490 --> 01:18:34.330
Joint attention is saying, you
are considering your image patch

01:18:34.330 --> 01:18:38.690
embeddings as inputs, but you're
also considering your text

01:18:38.690 --> 01:18:42.970
embeddings as input as well.

01:18:42.970 --> 01:18:48.130
And what you're saying is
that both these modalities

01:18:48.130 --> 01:18:52.090
are being attended, if you
want, through the self-attention

01:18:52.090 --> 01:18:53.770
layer.

01:18:53.770 --> 01:18:58.790
So continuing the analogy, so
let's say, sorry, I'm a painter.

01:18:58.790 --> 01:19:00.430
And let's say
Shervine is a poet.

01:19:00.430 --> 01:19:02.570
So instead of
Shervine writing to me

01:19:02.570 --> 01:19:07.610
exactly what needs to be
drawn, now, both Shervine and I

01:19:07.610 --> 01:19:10.930
are in the same room,
and we're talking

01:19:10.930 --> 01:19:15.930
about how things should be
placed with respect to the text.

01:19:15.930 --> 01:19:18.610
And let's suppose
if I paint something

01:19:18.610 --> 01:19:21.640
and then, I don't
know, I need to write

01:19:21.640 --> 01:19:25.880
Stanford top right and then
my drawing is in such a way

01:19:25.880 --> 01:19:29.440
that there is not
that much space,

01:19:29.440 --> 01:19:31.900
then we can talk amongst
one another to say,

01:19:31.900 --> 01:19:34.880
OK, actually, I want Stanford
to be written in maybe smaller

01:19:34.880 --> 01:19:42.000
fonts so we can change what we
are each saying as a constraint

01:19:42.000 --> 01:19:46.880
and doing as an image, as
a function of one another.

01:19:46.880 --> 01:19:49.720
So in other words,
cross-attention

01:19:49.720 --> 01:19:53.880
is considering text
more as being a given.

01:19:53.880 --> 01:19:56.720
So it's OK.

01:19:56.720 --> 01:19:58.500
So you have a patch embedding.

01:19:58.500 --> 01:20:01.820
Look at which piece of
text is relevant for you,

01:20:01.820 --> 01:20:04.920
and just do something.

01:20:04.920 --> 01:20:07.480
And joint attention
is saying, OK,

01:20:07.480 --> 01:20:15.720
let's consider both, and try to
find a representation of each,

01:20:15.720 --> 01:20:18.950
such that we can get to our
final destination in the best

01:20:18.950 --> 01:20:19.730
way possible.

01:20:23.230 --> 01:20:24.850
So I hope this makes sense.

01:20:24.850 --> 01:20:30.150
All of that to say that
nowadays most people do

01:20:30.150 --> 01:20:34.670
joint attention-related
methods, and in particular, I'm

01:20:34.670 --> 01:20:38.090
going to introduce a
term in one second.

01:20:38.090 --> 01:20:40.230
But before I do that, it's
exactly what I mentioned

01:20:40.230 --> 01:20:41.950
here-- the joint attention.

01:20:41.950 --> 01:20:46.230
We're considering the patch
embeddings and the text

01:20:46.230 --> 01:20:51.110
embeddings all jointly that goes
through this attention layer

01:20:51.110 --> 01:20:53.830
and so on.

01:20:53.830 --> 01:20:58.350
And the name of
such an architecture

01:20:58.350 --> 01:21:00.805
was coined as being an MM-DiT.

01:21:00.805 --> 01:21:02.930
So I don't exactly know
how you would pronounce it,

01:21:02.930 --> 01:21:07.310
but I just go by MM-DiT--
so Multi-Modal Diffusion

01:21:07.310 --> 01:21:09.390
Transformer.

01:21:09.390 --> 01:21:11.750
And this term was
coined in a paper

01:21:11.750 --> 01:21:15.190
that's actually not that long
ago-- so Stable Diffusion 3.

01:21:15.190 --> 01:21:19.390
That was published two
years ago-- so in 2024.

01:21:19.390 --> 01:21:23.190
And this relies on
a joint attention

01:21:23.190 --> 01:21:26.350
of different modalities.

01:21:26.350 --> 01:21:32.470
Now, there is another dimension
that some papers play around

01:21:32.470 --> 01:21:37.590
with, which is whether you
want to consider each modality

01:21:37.590 --> 01:21:42.750
separately or jointly.

01:21:42.750 --> 01:21:46.390
And in particular, you know
in the diffusion transformer

01:21:46.390 --> 01:21:49.030
architecture, you have this
feed-forward neural network

01:21:49.030 --> 01:21:50.290
where you project.

01:21:50.290 --> 01:21:52.870
You do some activation
function, and you project again

01:21:52.870 --> 01:21:57.270
where you learn different
ways to just represent

01:21:57.270 --> 01:21:58.710
the same thing.

01:21:58.710 --> 01:22:06.350
And if you were to consider
all modalities as going

01:22:06.350 --> 01:22:08.590
through the same thing--

01:22:08.590 --> 01:22:13.820
so just continuing the analogy
of the writer and the poet,

01:22:13.820 --> 01:22:16.740
it would be as if
you were saying

01:22:16.740 --> 01:22:19.760
that both the poet
and the-- sorry,

01:22:19.760 --> 01:22:25.780
both the poet and the painter,
they use the same tools.

01:22:25.780 --> 01:22:28.580
So you learn the
same projections.

01:22:28.580 --> 01:22:33.040
But if you were to consider
each modality on their own,

01:22:33.040 --> 01:22:37.297
if you were to consider
specific projection,

01:22:37.297 --> 01:22:38.880
depending on which
modality you're on,

01:22:38.880 --> 01:22:41.540
you're actually
learning tools that

01:22:41.540 --> 01:22:44.960
are tailored to the task
of whoever's doing it.

01:22:44.960 --> 01:22:47.660
So the painter will
have a great brush

01:22:47.660 --> 01:22:49.780
that is tailored to
whatever they're doing,

01:22:49.780 --> 01:22:53.460
and the poet will have
exactly whatever is tailored

01:22:53.460 --> 01:22:56.060
to their needs.

01:22:56.060 --> 01:22:58.200
Maybe I'm pushing it too
far with this analogy,

01:22:58.200 --> 01:23:01.980
but all of that to say that
we have two different classes

01:23:01.980 --> 01:23:03.960
of MM-DiT.

01:23:03.960 --> 01:23:06.080
One is single stream.

01:23:06.080 --> 01:23:08.300
So everything is
treated equally.

01:23:08.300 --> 01:23:10.700
And the second one
is double stream.

01:23:10.700 --> 01:23:14.500
Each modality has
its own stream.

01:23:14.500 --> 01:23:16.480
And you even have papers
that mixes the two.

01:23:16.480 --> 01:23:18.580
So I call them hybrids here.

01:23:18.580 --> 01:23:23.340
So they contain both single
stream and double stream layers.

01:23:23.340 --> 01:23:29.640
And the next few slides are just
about calling out such models.

01:23:29.640 --> 01:23:34.520
So the state of the art models
that you would see nowadays,

01:23:34.520 --> 01:23:37.120
they actually are relying
on this architecture.

01:23:37.120 --> 01:23:41.900
So I'm going to just mention
a double stream MM-DiT.

01:23:41.900 --> 01:23:43.860
So this one is an
image for those of you

01:23:43.860 --> 01:23:46.740
who have heard of it.

01:23:46.740 --> 01:23:50.240
Then you have Z-image, as an
example of a single stream--

01:23:50.240 --> 01:23:54.460
so here treating
everything as one.

01:23:54.460 --> 01:23:56.720
And then for those of
you who have heard of it,

01:23:56.720 --> 01:24:03.100
flux one contexts is
actually doing a bit of both.

01:24:03.100 --> 01:24:06.900
So I put all the
links in the slides.

01:24:06.900 --> 01:24:09.640
So we don't have time to talk
about each of these papers.

01:24:09.640 --> 01:24:11.850
But if you're interested,
you can just go there

01:24:11.850 --> 01:24:17.210
and just look at the papers
to see how they design it.

01:24:17.210 --> 01:24:19.790
It is not in the
scope of the finals,

01:24:19.790 --> 01:24:21.590
so don't worry if
you don't go there.

01:24:21.590 --> 01:24:23.050
It's not required.

01:24:23.050 --> 01:24:28.050
But if you're interested,
I think it's a good source.

01:24:28.050 --> 01:24:30.770
And just continuing
the timeline.

01:24:30.770 --> 01:24:36.050
So nowadays,
image-generation models

01:24:36.050 --> 01:24:40.130
are full of MM-DiT-based models.

01:24:40.130 --> 01:24:43.290
And in particular, if you
think about when the diffusion

01:24:43.290 --> 01:24:47.210
transformer was published,
which was in 2022,

01:24:47.210 --> 01:24:52.370
Stable Diffusion 3, which was
the first one to coin MM-DiT

01:24:52.370 --> 01:24:54.030
was released in 2024.

01:24:54.030 --> 01:24:57.830
And then the models I mentioned
were released in 2025.

01:24:57.830 --> 01:25:00.670
And again, the ones that
are being published in 2026,

01:25:00.670 --> 01:25:02.210
they seem to follow
the same trend.

01:25:02.210 --> 01:25:07.250
All of that to say that I
think these are relevant

01:25:07.250 --> 01:25:11.830
architectures that I think
are good to have in mind.

01:25:11.830 --> 01:25:12.990
I'm looking at the time.

01:25:12.990 --> 01:25:17.110
I'm slightly over time,
but any questions on that?

01:25:21.050 --> 01:25:23.130
We're all good?

01:25:23.130 --> 01:25:23.850
Perfect.

01:25:23.850 --> 01:25:26.970
So with that, I'm going
to give it to Shervine.

01:25:26.970 --> 01:25:28.490
Thank you, Afshine.

01:25:28.490 --> 01:25:31.330
So with all these
models, we have

01:25:31.330 --> 01:25:35.330
seen that one constant is
that we need to represent

01:25:35.330 --> 01:25:37.250
the position of the input.

01:25:37.250 --> 01:25:40.490
So the next section here
is called optimization.

01:25:40.490 --> 01:25:43.610
But we're not going to look at
optimizations of everything--

01:25:43.610 --> 01:25:47.410
just the optimization
of the position.

01:25:47.410 --> 01:25:50.410
So in order to
make things simple,

01:25:50.410 --> 01:25:53.050
I'm going to start
in the 1D case.

01:25:53.050 --> 01:25:56.890
And then we're going to see
how we can adapt this concept

01:25:56.890 --> 01:25:59.690
of positions in the 2D one.

01:25:59.690 --> 01:26:04.010
So as you recall, the
original transformer

01:26:04.010 --> 01:26:06.800
was based on text tokens.

01:26:06.800 --> 01:26:09.640
So in this case, where
you have the sentence,

01:26:09.640 --> 01:26:11.640
a teddy bear is
reading, what you

01:26:11.640 --> 01:26:16.440
would want is when you do
comparisons between your tokens

01:26:16.440 --> 01:26:20.320
in the attention layer,
that the token teddy

01:26:20.320 --> 01:26:26.680
bear or the representation of it
is seen as being close to tokens

01:26:26.680 --> 01:26:32.320
around it or itself and far from
others that are far from it.

01:26:32.320 --> 01:26:34.560
That's the goal.

01:26:34.560 --> 01:26:38.560
So let's write that
in mathematical form.

01:26:38.560 --> 01:26:44.520
So what we want is a way for
us to compare two embeddings

01:26:44.520 --> 01:26:47.680
and when you do an operation
like the dot product

01:26:47.680 --> 01:26:51.440
to have it as a function
of the distance.

01:26:51.440 --> 01:26:53.560
But here I'm saying
the dot product

01:26:53.560 --> 01:26:57.280
of embeddings of these
tokens it's not totally true.

01:26:57.280 --> 01:26:59.840
Here I'm going to
put it in quotes,

01:26:59.840 --> 01:27:01.680
because in the
case of attention,

01:27:01.680 --> 01:27:04.120
we're not comparing
the same embeddings.

01:27:04.120 --> 01:27:07.240
We're comparing
the query embedding

01:27:07.240 --> 01:27:10.960
with the key embedding
of the other token.

01:27:10.960 --> 01:27:15.440
So it's modulo some
transformation.

01:27:15.440 --> 01:27:19.680
And just to write
things down, we

01:27:19.680 --> 01:27:23.040
are going to decompose,
as a first approach,

01:27:23.040 --> 01:27:27.320
the representation of the
embedding of a given token

01:27:27.320 --> 01:27:30.800
with what we call the input
embedding, which is whatever

01:27:30.800 --> 01:27:35.000
representation we already have
that is not position-specific,

01:27:35.000 --> 01:27:38.760
and then adding it as
an element-wise addition

01:27:38.760 --> 01:27:41.540
in the vector space,
the position embedding.

01:27:41.540 --> 01:27:44.360
That's what the original
transformer paper proposed,

01:27:44.360 --> 01:27:49.760
such that when you do the dot
product operation that I just

01:27:49.760 --> 01:27:55.000
mentioned, you have
a term that pops out

01:27:55.000 --> 01:27:59.800
that is a dot product of these
position embeddings together.

01:27:59.800 --> 01:28:05.710
And I'm not saying that all the
other terms are not functions

01:28:05.710 --> 01:28:07.170
of this position embeddings.

01:28:07.170 --> 01:28:09.710
Some of them are cross
terms, but we're just

01:28:09.710 --> 01:28:11.910
going to ignore them for now.

01:28:11.910 --> 01:28:16.710
And we're going to focus on what
choice to get for these position

01:28:16.710 --> 01:28:21.870
embeddings in order for the
representation to make sense.

01:28:21.870 --> 01:28:27.350
So the original transformer
hard coded it with a formula

01:28:27.350 --> 01:28:29.070
that I just wrote down here.

01:28:29.070 --> 01:28:30.210
So it might look scary.

01:28:30.210 --> 01:28:33.710
Some of you might know about it
already, but for all the others,

01:28:33.710 --> 01:28:35.470
it might look scary.

01:28:35.470 --> 01:28:39.310
So you have a combination
of sines and cosines

01:28:39.310 --> 01:28:43.190
that you might ask
yourself, why are they here?

01:28:43.190 --> 01:28:46.790
So we're going to decompose
all the notations in there

01:28:46.790 --> 01:28:50.870
and see intuitively
what they are for.

01:28:50.870 --> 01:28:56.230
So you see that you have
the position as a numerator

01:28:56.230 --> 01:28:57.890
within these sines and cosines.

01:28:57.890 --> 01:29:03.750
And on the denominator,
you have some huge number

01:29:03.750 --> 01:29:05.910
put at some exponent.

01:29:05.910 --> 01:29:11.870
And that exponent is a function
of the position in the vector

01:29:11.870 --> 01:29:14.790
and the dimension
of the embedding

01:29:14.790 --> 01:29:17.390
altogether, such
that when you vary

01:29:17.390 --> 01:29:22.230
this I between the first
and the middle position,

01:29:22.230 --> 01:29:25.550
you cover exponents from 0 to 1.

01:29:25.550 --> 01:29:28.150
So in other words,
you have these signs

01:29:28.150 --> 01:29:32.790
that have frequencies
that are either high

01:29:32.790 --> 01:29:37.030
when this I is close
to 0 because you

01:29:37.030 --> 01:29:39.490
have the position over
something that is small.

01:29:39.490 --> 01:29:41.770
So it's going to
vary quite fast.

01:29:41.770 --> 01:29:44.950
So we're going to call
them high frequency.

01:29:44.950 --> 01:29:47.950
And on the other
end of the spectrum,

01:29:47.950 --> 01:29:52.870
when you have I close to the
dimension of the vector over 2,

01:29:52.870 --> 01:29:55.310
it's going to be close to 1.

01:29:55.310 --> 01:29:57.990
And as you can see,
the number that

01:29:57.990 --> 01:30:02.620
is elevated to that
exponent is some number

01:30:02.620 --> 01:30:06.660
that looks arbitrary,
but it's quite large.

01:30:06.660 --> 01:30:09.860
It's voluntarily taken
quite large with respect

01:30:09.860 --> 01:30:15.060
to the sequence length,
because the mindset behind it

01:30:15.060 --> 01:30:21.900
is that we want these
frequencies when I is high,

01:30:21.900 --> 01:30:23.980
to be considered as low.

01:30:23.980 --> 01:30:28.460
So in other words, when you have
a sequence length of let's say

01:30:28.460 --> 01:30:34.940
500, we want these 1 over
10,000 to be so small that

01:30:34.940 --> 01:30:41.060
the variation of that sine and
cosine is of low frequency with

01:30:41.060 --> 01:30:44.820
respect to the sequence length.

01:30:44.820 --> 01:30:47.320
Does that make sense?

01:30:47.320 --> 01:30:47.820
OK.

01:30:47.820 --> 01:30:48.540
Awesome.

01:30:48.540 --> 01:30:51.980
So now I'm going to show
you the exact same figure

01:30:51.980 --> 01:30:55.100
that Afshine showed
in a different context

01:30:55.100 --> 01:30:57.180
in a timestep embedding.

01:30:57.180 --> 01:30:59.460
But it turns out
that here we choose

01:30:59.460 --> 01:31:01.440
a similar mathematical framing.

01:31:01.440 --> 01:31:03.900
So the same graph applies.

01:31:03.900 --> 01:31:08.820
And we're going to analyze
parts of these projection

01:31:08.820 --> 01:31:12.860
and then see how it compares
with respect to our frequencies

01:31:12.860 --> 01:31:16.140
that we qualified
as high and low.

01:31:16.140 --> 01:31:20.260
So on the left, we're
looking at dimensions that

01:31:20.260 --> 01:31:22.400
are early on in the vector.

01:31:22.400 --> 01:31:28.260
So we call them the
high frequencies.

01:31:28.260 --> 01:31:32.700
And as you can see, when you
vary the sequence position,

01:31:32.700 --> 01:31:37.420
these numbers, they vary between
negative 1 and 1 quite fast.

01:31:37.420 --> 01:31:40.060
And on the other end
of the spectrum, when

01:31:40.060 --> 01:31:42.740
you look at positions
of this vector

01:31:42.740 --> 01:31:50.220
that are larger than the cosine
and sines take time in order

01:31:50.220 --> 01:31:54.650
to cover the whole face.

01:31:54.650 --> 01:32:01.370
So now I talked about a given
frequency that was hard coded.

01:32:01.370 --> 01:32:03.310
This is a design choice.

01:32:03.310 --> 01:32:06.170
We're going to hide
it under some variable

01:32:06.170 --> 01:32:08.750
that I'm going to call omega y.

01:32:11.580 --> 01:32:16.690
And we're going to look side
by side what these vectors look

01:32:16.690 --> 01:32:21.450
like when you take position
m on one hand and position n.

01:32:21.450 --> 01:32:23.690
And we're going
to try to see what

01:32:23.690 --> 01:32:25.850
taking the dot products
between both of them

01:32:25.850 --> 01:32:26.990
is going to give us.

01:32:29.762 --> 01:32:33.970
So here you see that you have
these sines that are aligned

01:32:33.970 --> 01:32:37.770
and these cosines, so that
when you do the dot product,

01:32:37.770 --> 01:32:45.370
you have a sum of these sines of
similar frequencies at position

01:32:45.370 --> 01:32:51.970
m and n and plus a product of
cosines of a same frequency

01:32:51.970 --> 01:32:55.090
at positions m and n again.

01:32:55.090 --> 01:32:59.210
And if you recall your
trigonometry class,

01:32:59.210 --> 01:33:07.050
sine A, sine B, plus cosine
A cosine B is an identity.

01:33:07.050 --> 01:33:12.370
And you can rewrite it as
cosine of A minus B, such

01:33:12.370 --> 01:33:15.890
that when you compute
this dot product,

01:33:15.890 --> 01:33:22.770
this is nothing else as the sum
of about dimension of embeddings

01:33:22.770 --> 01:33:29.090
over 2 terms of this cosine
of difference of terms.

01:33:29.090 --> 01:33:32.090
So it's quite interpretable.

01:33:32.090 --> 01:33:37.050
So now let's take a look at
when we compare positions

01:33:37.050 --> 01:33:41.810
m and n what the dot product
evolves with respect to time.

01:33:41.810 --> 01:33:47.570
So you see that it has
this natural curve that

01:33:47.570 --> 01:33:48.950
overall decreases.

01:33:48.950 --> 01:33:50.360
But it's not monotonic.

01:33:50.360 --> 01:33:52.700
It doesn't
monotonically decrease,

01:33:52.700 --> 01:33:54.840
but it tends to decrease.

01:33:54.840 --> 01:33:59.680
And you can see multiple
regions in this curve

01:33:59.680 --> 01:34:02.120
that you can interpret
as to why you

01:34:02.120 --> 01:34:04.340
have such phenomenon happening.

01:34:04.340 --> 01:34:08.760
So at the very beginning,
you see a sudden drop.

01:34:08.760 --> 01:34:11.120
And this can be
interpreted by the fact

01:34:11.120 --> 01:34:14.120
that all your
cosines in your sum,

01:34:14.120 --> 01:34:16.800
whether high frequency
or low frequency,

01:34:16.800 --> 01:34:19.960
start from an angle
close to 0, and they

01:34:19.960 --> 01:34:24.840
all drop from these
cosine of 0 equals 1.

01:34:24.840 --> 01:34:27.480
And then as you
progress, when you

01:34:27.480 --> 01:34:30.920
increase the distance
between positions, m and n,

01:34:30.920 --> 01:34:33.920
you have your high
frequency cosine

01:34:33.920 --> 01:34:37.660
terms that oscillate
between minus 1 and 1,

01:34:37.660 --> 01:34:41.840
and they will tend to
cancel each other out.

01:34:41.840 --> 01:34:47.840
So this is why you see some
oscillations but not that much.

01:34:47.840 --> 01:34:53.210
And then later on you see more
and more oscillations coming in.

01:34:53.210 --> 01:34:57.990
This is your low frequency
terms that start oscillating,

01:34:57.990 --> 01:35:03.390
and this is how it
can be interpreted.

01:35:03.390 --> 01:35:06.910
So why are we choosing
these positions embeddings

01:35:06.910 --> 01:35:08.510
in the first place?

01:35:08.510 --> 01:35:12.550
So we saw that we can have
an interesting interpretation

01:35:12.550 --> 01:35:16.230
in terms of comparing two
position embeddings when

01:35:16.230 --> 01:35:18.330
they are close versus
far from each other.

01:35:18.330 --> 01:35:21.310
You have an intuitive
interpretation

01:35:21.310 --> 01:35:26.710
as to the dot products that can
be compared to the distance.

01:35:26.710 --> 01:35:29.350
And also something
that I haven't put here

01:35:29.350 --> 01:35:33.830
and that was highlighted
by initial authors,

01:35:33.830 --> 01:35:40.150
the position embedding when you
increase the position by a given

01:35:40.150 --> 01:35:44.150
factor, k, can be represented
by a linear operation.

01:35:44.150 --> 01:35:47.070
So when you have all these
matrix multiplication

01:35:47.070 --> 01:35:50.350
operations, you can
see that operation

01:35:50.350 --> 01:35:54.170
as being absorbed by
these matrix computations,

01:35:54.170 --> 01:35:58.030
such that position information
can be taken into account.

01:35:58.030 --> 01:36:01.550
So it has this linear
interpretation as well.

01:36:01.550 --> 01:36:07.310
And the argument is that
it's very simple because you

01:36:07.310 --> 01:36:09.130
have a pre-computed formula.

01:36:09.130 --> 01:36:11.310
So let's say at
training time, you

01:36:11.310 --> 01:36:15.670
have a given constraint
on the sequence length.

01:36:15.670 --> 01:36:18.230
If at inference time you
change that parameter,

01:36:18.230 --> 01:36:23.190
then you're not lost
because you know how

01:36:23.190 --> 01:36:26.550
to extrapolate by the formula.

01:36:26.550 --> 01:36:31.830
And so we see that it just
works well in practice.

01:36:31.830 --> 01:36:36.150
But then on the flip side, and
I'm not talking about the fact

01:36:36.150 --> 01:36:39.990
that it's hard-coded, but more
about the absolute property

01:36:39.990 --> 01:36:43.470
of these position
embeddings, we saw

01:36:43.470 --> 01:36:46.900
that it was introduced
at the input level,

01:36:46.900 --> 01:36:51.780
whereas the initial
motivation where I mentioned

01:36:51.780 --> 01:36:54.260
position embeddings
were helpful were more

01:36:54.260 --> 01:36:56.900
at the attention level.

01:36:56.900 --> 01:36:59.660
So it seems like we're injecting
this position information

01:36:59.660 --> 01:37:04.620
at a place where that
information doesn't matter most,

01:37:04.620 --> 01:37:07.220
but it might matter
most at places

01:37:07.220 --> 01:37:09.460
where you want to
compare embeddings

01:37:09.460 --> 01:37:11.040
with respect to one another.

01:37:11.040 --> 01:37:15.100
So we're going to see
how to alleviate that.

01:37:15.100 --> 01:37:19.700
And I mentioned as well this
extra interaction terms.

01:37:19.700 --> 01:37:23.500
When you add position embeddings
at the very beginning,

01:37:23.500 --> 01:37:27.540
that might interact with
the input embeddings.

01:37:27.540 --> 01:37:30.220
And I'm not saying
this is bad, but I'm

01:37:30.220 --> 01:37:33.020
saying this might not
be the intention we

01:37:33.020 --> 01:37:35.540
have behind this
position embeddings.

01:37:35.540 --> 01:37:38.260
So it doesn't seem to align
with the design choice

01:37:38.260 --> 01:37:40.620
that we have in mind.

01:37:40.620 --> 01:37:45.980
And just to give you some
information regarding who

01:37:45.980 --> 01:37:48.080
used it, basically, everyone.

01:37:48.080 --> 01:37:50.600
Like every first
paper on transformers

01:37:50.600 --> 01:37:53.740
used absolute position
embedding-- so not necessarily

01:37:53.740 --> 01:37:57.940
the hard-coded version, but the
one that adds it in the input.

01:37:57.940 --> 01:38:02.300
So the transformer, of course,
did it the way I presented.

01:38:02.300 --> 01:38:05.240
The original VIT
didn't hardcode it.

01:38:05.240 --> 01:38:07.900
It was something
that was learned.

01:38:07.900 --> 01:38:13.860
And then the DiT did hard-code
it but in 2D space in a manner

01:38:13.860 --> 01:38:17.460
similar to one that we're
going to see very soon.

01:38:17.460 --> 01:38:21.500
But it did so at
the input level.

01:38:21.500 --> 01:38:23.960
Yeah.

01:38:23.960 --> 01:38:24.560
A great point.

01:38:24.560 --> 01:38:25.920
So what about the 2D case?

01:38:25.920 --> 01:38:28.300
We're going to see it.

01:38:28.300 --> 01:38:29.560
And yeah, great point.

01:38:33.565 --> 01:38:35.420
But first before
doing that, we're

01:38:35.420 --> 01:38:37.780
going to see if there
is a better location

01:38:37.780 --> 01:38:40.830
to have these position
embeddings be represented.

01:38:40.830 --> 01:38:44.170
And as you might have
guessed from all my hints,

01:38:44.170 --> 01:38:47.730
the attention layer is going
to be the place to put them.

01:38:47.730 --> 01:38:54.750
And this is the key insight from
RoPE Rotary Position Embeddings.

01:38:54.750 --> 01:38:58.650
That was a paper in
2021 that introduced

01:38:58.650 --> 01:39:01.790
these kinds of
representation of positions,

01:39:01.790 --> 01:39:06.130
and that is the leading way
people do it in practice today.

01:39:06.130 --> 01:39:09.090
And the way it
works is as follows.

01:39:09.090 --> 01:39:10.810
You take queries.

01:39:10.810 --> 01:39:12.330
You take keys.

01:39:12.330 --> 01:39:16.910
And instead of fitting them
in the dot product as is,

01:39:16.910 --> 01:39:21.250
you will rotate them by a
rotation operation that takes

01:39:21.250 --> 01:39:23.690
into account their position.

01:39:23.690 --> 01:39:24.710
So you take the query.

01:39:24.710 --> 01:39:28.530
You rotate it with respect to
something that depends on m.

01:39:28.530 --> 01:39:29.810
Same for key.

01:39:29.810 --> 01:39:32.570
That depends on n, its position.

01:39:32.570 --> 01:39:37.890
And you're going to do the dot
product of these rotated queries

01:39:37.890 --> 01:39:40.930
and keys instead
of the raw ones.

01:39:40.930 --> 01:39:43.370
And one nice property
that is interpretable

01:39:43.370 --> 01:39:47.090
here is that you see
a rotation matrix that

01:39:47.090 --> 01:39:52.510
depends on the difference of
positions, n minus m, pop out.

01:39:52.510 --> 01:39:56.430
So you feel like it's a design
choice that that makes sense.

01:40:00.130 --> 01:40:05.030
And then to answer your
points, now we're good for 1D.

01:40:05.030 --> 01:40:07.290
But what about 2D?

01:40:07.290 --> 01:40:11.010
So 2D you have this coordinates.

01:40:11.010 --> 01:40:13.790
But if you don't talk
about coordinates just yet,

01:40:13.790 --> 01:40:16.690
you might number
them in a 1D manner.

01:40:16.690 --> 01:40:22.210
And it's not super clear whether
it makes sense at all to compare

01:40:22.210 --> 01:40:25.410
1D numbers with
respect to one another.

01:40:25.410 --> 01:40:29.390
So you have a few
ways to get around it.

01:40:29.390 --> 01:40:33.490
So the first idea, which
is a similar flavor of what

01:40:33.490 --> 01:40:39.880
the original DiT paper
used is to segregate axes,

01:40:39.880 --> 01:40:43.280
such that you represent
vectors with respect

01:40:43.280 --> 01:40:46.640
to their coordinates
in the 2D space.

01:40:46.640 --> 01:40:51.400
And you do the x-axis in
some part of the vector

01:40:51.400 --> 01:40:54.680
and the y-axis at
some other part.

01:40:54.680 --> 01:40:57.280
And here we're talking
about one given

01:40:57.280 --> 01:41:02.960
design choice of how to order
the indices with respect

01:41:02.960 --> 01:41:04.640
to where you project.

01:41:04.640 --> 01:41:08.180
But it doesn't have to be
grouped by even or odd indices.

01:41:08.180 --> 01:41:10.040
You can group these
by any way you

01:41:10.040 --> 01:41:13.440
want because this is arbitrary.

01:41:13.440 --> 01:41:16.560
And so this is one
way of doing it.

01:41:16.560 --> 01:41:19.920
It's called axial RoPE.

01:41:19.920 --> 01:41:23.640
There is another way that
you could get around it

01:41:23.640 --> 01:41:26.720
that mitigates the fact that--

01:41:26.720 --> 01:41:29.440
so here if you do
the actual way,

01:41:29.440 --> 01:41:33.600
one thing is that if you do
the dot product between two

01:41:33.600 --> 01:41:37.160
rotated vectors, you realize
that you're comparing

01:41:37.160 --> 01:41:39.360
x with x and y with y.

01:41:39.360 --> 01:41:43.080
So you're missing that
interaction component.

01:41:43.080 --> 01:41:49.400
And one way you can go around it
is to mix rotations with respect

01:41:49.400 --> 01:41:52.200
to x and y in the same vector.

01:41:52.200 --> 01:41:57.400
So this is the
so-called mixed RoPE.

01:41:57.400 --> 01:42:01.880
And the authors of the paper
that discuss these two 2D

01:42:01.880 --> 01:42:09.920
strategies made a representation
of a given input position

01:42:09.920 --> 01:42:14.902
and applied a frequency-based
technique that I know some

01:42:14.902 --> 01:42:17.360
of you might know from the
physics space called the Fourier

01:42:17.360 --> 01:42:18.180
Transform.

01:42:18.180 --> 01:42:21.520
So to apply the
Fourier Transform,

01:42:21.520 --> 01:42:26.520
it's filtered out for a
given frequency's budget--

01:42:26.520 --> 01:42:29.480
only those that were
taken into consideration.

01:42:29.480 --> 01:42:32.240
And after trying to
reconstruct the signal

01:42:32.240 --> 01:42:36.190
from these set of
frequencies, saw

01:42:36.190 --> 01:42:40.550
that Axial RoPE had all
these axial artifacts that

01:42:40.550 --> 01:42:44.190
were due to this lack of
interaction between the two

01:42:44.190 --> 01:42:48.790
axes, whereas Mixed RoPE
was able to leverage

01:42:48.790 --> 01:42:51.710
the same set of frequencies,
like the same set

01:42:51.710 --> 01:42:55.210
of frequencies budgets in
a way more efficient way.

01:42:58.250 --> 01:43:00.150
So these are two ways
of looking at them,

01:43:00.150 --> 01:43:04.270
and mixing things seems
to make it better.

01:43:04.270 --> 01:43:09.230
And something else that you
might worry about beyond taking

01:43:09.230 --> 01:43:13.830
into account the 2D case, is how
to make sense of these positions

01:43:13.830 --> 01:43:18.490
when you have different number
of patches presented to you.

01:43:18.490 --> 01:43:20.430
So, for example, let's
say your teddy bear

01:43:20.430 --> 01:43:24.150
is in low resolution
versus high resolution.

01:43:24.150 --> 01:43:27.230
How does it make
sense me as a model

01:43:27.230 --> 01:43:30.270
to interpret what is at the
center of the image versus what

01:43:30.270 --> 01:43:32.830
is on the left or
what is on the right?

01:43:32.830 --> 01:43:38.270
And this is what
Seedream 2 thought about.

01:43:38.270 --> 01:43:43.910
And the idea here is to
center the coordinates

01:43:43.910 --> 01:43:46.270
in a way that will
tell the model where

01:43:46.270 --> 01:43:50.030
the center of the image is
in order for it to locate

01:43:50.030 --> 01:43:51.670
different parts of the image.

01:43:51.670 --> 01:43:57.470
So instead of having like
a grid that starts from 0,

01:43:57.470 --> 01:44:01.950
you have the 0 roughly being
at the center of the image,

01:44:01.950 --> 01:44:05.270
and then you adjust
the external points.

01:44:05.270 --> 01:44:12.110
And one thing that you might
wonder by Afshine's description

01:44:12.110 --> 01:44:15.070
of MM-DiT models,
for example, those

01:44:15.070 --> 01:44:17.590
that perform joint
attention, so you

01:44:17.590 --> 01:44:20.670
have these points
in the architecture

01:44:20.670 --> 01:44:25.150
where you might have text tokens
interacting with image tokens.

01:44:25.150 --> 01:44:27.310
So one question you
might ask yourself,

01:44:27.310 --> 01:44:31.580
how to quantify the position of
one with respect to the other.

01:44:31.580 --> 01:44:35.780
And by thinking
about this, one thing

01:44:35.780 --> 01:44:39.860
you might want to think
about is what wouldn't work.

01:44:39.860 --> 01:44:42.860
So what wouldn't
work intuitively

01:44:42.860 --> 01:44:49.420
is the positions at the
horizontal lines of the image

01:44:49.420 --> 01:44:51.420
and the vertical ones.

01:44:51.420 --> 01:44:54.540
Because intuitively, you
do not want your model

01:44:54.540 --> 01:44:57.060
to think that your
text tokens are

01:44:57.060 --> 01:44:59.500
a continuation of your image.

01:44:59.500 --> 01:45:04.660
So one natural position that
the coin image paper points out

01:45:04.660 --> 01:45:07.780
is the diagonal, because
it's clear that it's out

01:45:07.780 --> 01:45:10.500
of the area of the image.

01:45:10.500 --> 01:45:17.780
And by doing so, they see that
they can just live together.

01:45:17.780 --> 01:45:23.780
And if you use an actual
formulation of the 2D

01:45:23.780 --> 01:45:29.060
representation, you even notice
that your text tokens become

01:45:29.060 --> 01:45:32.460
equivalent to the 1D case
because you have your x

01:45:32.460 --> 01:45:36.020
and y's that are incremented
by 1 between one another.

01:45:36.020 --> 01:45:39.580
So just you fall back
on the same case.

01:45:39.580 --> 01:45:45.860
And so I just went through
some of the keys that made some

01:45:45.860 --> 01:45:48.100
of these techniques work well.

01:45:48.100 --> 01:45:53.260
I just want to say that this
is very much an open problem.

01:45:53.260 --> 01:45:58.180
There is no many papers that use
the same techniques but rather

01:45:58.180 --> 01:46:03.280
many that use variations to
reach their own architectures,

01:46:03.280 --> 01:46:05.480
goals.

01:46:05.480 --> 01:46:08.500
And then there are
trade offs as well.

01:46:08.500 --> 01:46:12.540
And you might remember
from the early slides

01:46:12.540 --> 01:46:17.460
that we were talking about teddy
bear that was wanting to dance.

01:46:17.460 --> 01:46:21.090
So here is our teddy bear
wishing you a great weekend.

